(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[72],{2270:function(e,t,n){"use strict";n.d(t,{n:function(){return r}});var r=[{slug:"spotyboys-from-recommendations-to-a-music-service",title:"SpotyBoys: Turning a Music Recommender into a Service",date:"2026-10-29",description:"Our spring-semester music recommendation project at NYU: session-based ranking, Navidrome playback, and the serving, monitoring and retraining work around the model.",tags:["ML Systems","Python","MLOps","Recommendations","NYU"],githubUrl:"https://github.com/Bobosi-coder/SpotyBoys",content:'<div class="spotyboys-guide">\n<p>During my spring semester at NYU Tandon, I worked on <strong>SpotyBoys</strong>, a music recommendation system, with <strong>Dawei Sun and Yonghyeon Shin</strong> for our Machine Learning System Engineering &amp; Operation class. We wanted to connect a recommendation model to something a person could actually use: choose music, listen, skip a track, and get another queue.</p>\n<p>I enjoyed working on the space between the model and the application. A ranked list of track IDs looks like a finished result until the player cannot find those tracks, the next request repeats the same artists, or the service quietly falls back while its health endpoint still says everything is fine.</p>\n<p>This post walks through the system we built together. My contributions included the serving stack, playback integration, deployment work, serving monitoring, promotion checks and a concurrent load-test harness. The recommender and training pipeline were team work; the <a href="https://github.com/Bobosi-coder/SpotyBoys/commits/main/">repository history</a> records contributions from all three of us.</p>\n<nav aria-label="In this article"><strong>In this article</strong><ul><li><a href="#problem">The problem beyond ranking songs</a></li><li><a href="#pipeline">How the recommender works</a></li><li><a href="#integration">Making recommendations playable</a></li><li><a href="#feedback">Closing the feedback loop</a></li><li><a href="#operations">Fallbacks, monitoring and promotion</a></li><li><a href="#lessons">What I learned and what remains</a></li></ul></nav>\n<h2 id="problem">The problem beyond ranking songs</h2>\n<p>The model answers a narrow question: given a listener’s history and current session, which candidate tracks should come next? The application has more obligations. It needs an authenticated user, a playable catalog, a consistent session, a player that reports what happened, and a way to update the model without treating every training run as ready to serve.</p>\n<p>We used <strong>Navidrome</strong> for the music player and UI, with patches for a Recommendations page and playback-event capture. A Python FastAPI service handles recommendations and application state. PostgreSQL stores durable records, Redis supports runtime state, and nginx provides the browser-facing entry point. Docker Compose assembles the service stack. GPU training lives separately, with MLflow tracking and an Airflow DAG that invokes training over SSH.</p>\n<figure><img src="/VishalJhaPortfolio/images/spotyboys/system.svg" width="1040" height="650" alt="SpotyBoys architecture: browser connects through nginx to Navidrome and the recommendation API; events enter PostgreSQL, are exported to object storage, and feed GPU retraining through Airflow. Reviewed model bundles return to serving."/><figcaption>Simplified architecture based on the repository. This is a system diagram, not a screenshot or a claim that the semester deployment is still online.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2 id="pipeline">Retrieve a small pool, then rank it</h2>\n<p>The pipeline separates candidate retrieval from ranking. Retrieval narrows the catalog to a manageable pool. Ranking spends more computation deciding the order within that pool. Policy filtering then makes the result suitable for playback.</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Offline artifacts:</strong> Item2Vec track embeddings, session and playlist co-occurrence matrices, user preference centroids and popularity scores.</li><li><strong>Candidate retrieval:</strong> tracks related to the recent session, tracks near the listener’s longer-term preferences, and popular tracks for coverage.</li><li><strong>Session ranking:</strong> a GRU uses the session prefix to score the candidates.</li><li><strong>Playback policy:</strong> remove unavailable, recently seen or disliked tracks and diversify the queue by artist.</li></ol>\n<p>The <a href="https://github.com/Bobosi-coder/SpotyBoys/blob/457c025ce77a99d9437ad374346458bee06aa8f7/src/retriever/retriever.py">retriever</a> allocates up to 100 candidates to co-occurrence, 80 to preference nearest neighbours and 20 to popularity, then deduplicates and caps the pool at 200. These are configured candidate limits, not measured throughput. Popularity also helps when a new listener has little history.</p>\n<p>The <a href="https://github.com/Bobosi-coder/SpotyBoys/blob/457c025ce77a99d9437ad374346458bee06aa8f7/src/ranker/model.py">ranker</a> uses 128-dimensional embeddings and a two-layer GRU. It combines a session representation, a long-term preference vector, a candidate embedding and three similarity features. An MLP produces the candidate’s score. The intuition is straightforward: a listener’s general taste matters, but so does what they are listening to right now.</p>\n<p>One implementation detail matters when describing this honestly: the model supports positive, neutral and skip labels, but the serving adapter at the reviewed revision passes the recent prefix to the real runtime with neutral labels. Playback feedback is collected for the data loop; that does not mean every live ranking request already makes full use of it.</p>\n<h2 id="integration">A track ID is not yet a playable song</h2>\n<p>The training artifacts and media player have different identifiers. A track can exist in an embedding table and still be absent from the music library, or lack a valid Navidrome mapping. Sending that ID to the browser does not produce music.</p>\n<p>The catalog-sync worker maps the library into a playable catalog. The serving pipeline intersects ranked IDs with that catalog, then the media integration uses the corresponding Navidrome identity. This makes the catalog mapping part of the recommendation contract. A model can load successfully and still return no usable songs if those namespaces disagree.</p>\n<p>I added a requirement for the full ML runtime so a deployment intended to exercise the real retriever and ranker would not silently substitute the lightweight fixture path. The repository retains fixtures for tests, but the serving mode can refuse to initialize without the required runtime. That distinction made debugging more concrete: “the API responded” and “the intended model ran” became separate checks. See the <a href="https://github.com/Bobosi-coder/SpotyBoys/blob/457c025ce77a99d9437ad374346458bee06aa8f7/packages/recommendation_engine/pipeline.py">serving pipeline</a>.</p>\n<p>Queue construction needed attention too. A list dominated by one artist, or a refresh that repeats recently recommended tracks, can be technically valid and still feel broken. Artist diversification, recent-track tracking and playback-triggered refreshes belong in the application behaviour around the score.</p>\n<h2 id="feedback">A listening session has a lifecycle</h2>\n<p>To retrain from usage, we needed to record what the listener actually did. A recommendation impression is different from a playback start, and a start is different from a completed listen.</p>\n<p>The Navidrome event bridge assigns a <code>playback_id</code> to a physical play and reuses it for the terminal skip or completion event. The backend can insert the start and update that same play instead of counting the lifecycle as unrelated listening events. The exported listening fraction supplies a training label. Those labels are useful proxies for engagement, not proof that a listener liked or disliked a song.</p>\n<p>A delta-export worker writes session and user-related Parquet files to object storage and records a checkpoint. Its configured loop defaults to once a day; the retraining trigger defaults to 1,000 new complete-play events. Airflow then invokes the GPU training pipeline. These values describe the implementation, not a claim that we had 1,000 daily active listeners.</p>\n<p>The training branch merges exported deltas with the original 30Music snapshot, rebuilds retrieval artifacts and trains the ranker. MLflow records runs, and a promotion step produces a versioned serving bundle. The important connection is that training produces an artifact the service knows how to load, with enough metadata to identify which version is active.</p>\n<h2 id="operations">A successful response can still hide a broken model</h2>\n<p>My monitoring work started with a simple problem: a service may return HTTP 200 while serving fallback recommendations on every request. A dashboard showing only uptime would miss the loss of personalization.</p>\n<p>I implemented explicit serving states for conditions such as a missing model, a pipeline exception, an insufficient playable catalog and an operator-selected degraded mode. The response carries the fallback state and reason. Where the catalog permits it, the service can return a deterministic playable fallback instead of failing the whole request.</p>\n<p>That is a trade-off. The listener gets a queue, but it may be less personalized. The system needs to expose that difference rather than report fallback as normal model performance. The <a href="https://github.com/Bobosi-coder/SpotyBoys/blob/457c025ce77a99d9437ad374346458bee06aa8f7/packages/recommendation_engine/serving_state.py">state decision code</a> and its tests make the behaviour inspectable.</p>\n<div class="spotyboys-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Evidence the service records</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Did the intended model serve this request?</td><td>Model version, fallback state, reason and pipeline error</td></tr><tr><td>Did it return usable recommendations?</td><td>Candidate, playable and returned-item counts</td></tr><tr><td>Was serving responsive?</td><td>Pipeline and total latency, aggregated into time windows</td></tr><tr><td>Is there enough evidence to act?</td><td>Request count and an explicit insufficient-sample state</td></tr><tr><td>Why was a model accepted or rejected?</td><td>Persisted promotion and rollback decisions</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Request telemetry is persisted in PostgreSQL and aggregated for Grafana. Telemetry-write exceptions are caught so an observability failure does not itself fail recommendation serving. The cost of that choice is that missing metrics must not be interpreted as a healthy service.</p>\n<p>The rollback evaluator waits for a minimum request count before evaluating thresholds. With too few requests, it records insufficient evidence. With enough evidence, it can recommend rollback or mark a degradation condition. <strong>It does not automatically swap the serving bundle:</strong> the checked-in worker leaves that mutation manual, even if the automatic-rollback flag is requested.</p>\n<h2>Training success and deployment approval are different decisions</h2>\n<p>On the GPU training branch, automatic promotion compares a composite ranking score and validation loss against the baseline. The configured gate accepts a composite score of at least 99% of baseline and a loss no more than 105% of baseline. These are engineering tolerances, not a statistical guarantee that users will prefer the candidate.</p>\n<p>The serving side separately checks bundle validity, an approval record and model-version consistency before activation. There is an explicit override option, so this is an operational guard with a documented escape hatch. It should not be described as an unbypassable security boundary. The two parts are visible in the <a href="https://github.com/Bobosi-coder/SpotyBoys/blob/4d5b7f961e0f01973c1c142b6d18aaa4649b166c/scripts/promote.py">training promotion script</a> and <a href="https://github.com/Bobosi-coder/SpotyBoys/blob/457c025ce77a99d9437ad374346458bee06aa8f7/jobs/promotion-gate/promotion_gate.py">serving activation gate</a>.</p>\n<h2 id="lessons">What I learned, and what I would improve</h2>\n<p>The most useful part of this project was learning to debug the connections: training IDs versus media IDs, browser authentication versus API authentication, model availability versus service availability, and a completed training run versus a deployable version.</p>\n<p>A concrete example came from the load-test harness. The browser-facing nginx path read a session cookie, while the initial test sent an Authorization header. The fix made the harness use the same cookie mechanism as the browser and added a bootstrap check. Otherwise, an authentication failure could be mistaken for a recommendation-serving performance problem.</p>\n<p>The repository includes tests for playable-only recommendations, event idempotency, fallback behaviour, insufficient monitoring samples and promotion checks. Those are useful contract tests. They are not a substitute for evaluating the trained model, measuring a representative workload or running the whole infrastructure. I am not claiming a production latency number or recommendation-quality improvement from the presence of those tests.</p>\n<p>For a next iteration, I would focus on carrying actual feedback labels into live ranking, retaining reproducible model-quality and load-test results, and completing a tested rollback execution path. I would also harden the classroom deployment’s authentication, TLS and administrative access before treating it as a public service.</p>\n<p>SpotyBoys gave me a practical reason to learn the engineering around ML: getting the right data into the model, the right artifacts into the service, and enough evidence out of the service to understand when it stopped doing what we intended.</p>\n<h2>Team and source</h2>\n<p><strong>Built with Dawei Sun and Yonghyeon Shin.</strong> This was a collaborative NYU Tandon spring 2026 project. The system described here belongs to the team; the sections on my contribution identify the serving and operations work reflected in my commits.</p>\n<p><a href="https://github.com/Bobosi-coder/SpotyBoys">Explore SpotyBoys on GitHub →</a></p>\n<p class="spotyboys-source-note">Technical review baseline: main at <code>457c025</code>, with GPU training at <code>4d5b7f9</code>. Source inspection for this retrospective took place on October 29, 2026; the historical cloud deployment and training runs were not rerun for this post. Further detail: <a href="https://github.com/Bobosi-coder/SpotyBoys/blob/457c025ce77a99d9437ad374346458bee06aa8f7/docs/serving_monitoring_implementation_report.tex">serving and monitoring implementation report</a>, <a href="https://github.com/Bobosi-coder/SpotyBoys/blob/457c025ce77a99d9437ad374346458bee06aa8f7/tests/test_serving_monitoring.py">monitoring tests</a>, and <a href="https://github.com/Bobosi-coder/SpotyBoys/blob/457c025ce77a99d9437ad374346458bee06aa8f7/scripts/load_test_serving.py">load-test harness</a>.</p>\n</div>'},{slug:"my-kitty-terminal-setup",title:"My Kitty Terminal Setup: The Config, the Tools, and the Rough Edges",date:"2026-10-27",description:"Reproduce my macOS terminal: Atom One Dark, MesloLGS NF, native Kitty splits, a custom tab bar, Zsh tools and Yazi Markdown previews—with the complete configuration download.",tags:["Kitty","macOS","Terminal","Zsh","Developer Tools"],content:'<div class="terminal-guide">\n<p>I spend a lot of time moving between code, build output, Markdown notes and remote machines. I wanted a terminal where those tasks felt connected: split a pane, read a file with formatting, find an old command, and tell at a glance when I had entered SSH.</p>\n<p>This is the configuration I use on my Mac: <strong>Kitty, Zsh, Atom One Dark, MesloLGS NF, and a small collection of command-line tools</strong>. There is no Powerlevel10k or Starship prompt in these files. The powerline shapes at the top belong to Kitty’s tab bar, not the shell prompt.</p>\n<p>This guide comes from inspecting my actual configuration and installed Kitty 0.48.2. It includes the complete files, portable executable paths and the rough edges I found while checking my old notes. It is a macOS guide; the appearance and shortcuts are not a promise of identical pixels on Linux or a different display.</p>\n\n<p><a class="terminal-download" href="/VishalJhaPortfolio/downloads/kitty-setup.zip" download>Download the complete configuration bundle →</a> <a href="https://github.com/vishaljha2121/VishalJhaPortfolio/tree/master/public/downloads/kitty-setup">Browse the files first</a></p>\n<nav aria-label="In this guide"><strong>In this guide</strong><ul><li><a href="#layers">What each layer does</a></li><li><a href="#install">Install and reproduce the setup</a></li><li><a href="#appearance">Font, palette and rendering</a></li><li><a href="#panes">Panes, tabs and the shortcut legend</a></li><li><a href="#shell">Shell tools and Markdown previews</a></li><li><a href="#ssh">The SSH colour switch</a></li><li><a href="#checks">Checks, caveats and troubleshooting</a></li></ul></nav>\n<figure><img src="/VishalJhaPortfolio/images/kitty-setup/configuration-map.svg" width="1000" height="520" alt="Configuration map showing Kitty as the window layer, Zsh as the shell, and the reading, navigation and Git tools below it." /><figcaption>Configuration map using my palette. This is an explanatory diagram, not a screenshot of a running terminal.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2 id="layers">1. What each layer does</h2><p>Kitty draws the window, glyphs, cursor, panes and tabs. Zsh interprets commands and sets up aliases. The individual programs produce the coloured output. Changing a Kitty theme does not install syntax highlighting, a file browser or a Git pager.</p>\n<div class="terminal-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>My choice</th><th>What it adds</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Terminal</td><td>Kitty 0.48.2</td><td>Native panes, top tabs, shell integration and a Python tab renderer</td></tr><tr><td>Shell</td><td>Zsh 5.9</td><td>Aliases, fzf bindings, zoxide and an SSH wrapper</td></tr><tr><td>Appearance</td><td>MesloLGS NF, 14 pt; Atom One Dark</td><td>Nerd Font glyphs, a dark palette and blue cursor</td></tr><tr><td>Reading</td><td>bat 0.26.1; Glow 2.1.2</td><td>Syntax-coloured files, man pages and rendered Markdown</td></tr><tr><td>Navigation</td><td>lsd 1.2.0; fzf 0.74.2; zoxide 0.10.0</td><td>File icons, fuzzy selection and directory jumping</td></tr><tr><td>File browser</td><td>Yazi 26.5.6; Piper revision 9014ed2</td><td>Markdown previews through Glow</td></tr><tr><td>Git / optional banner</td><td>delta 0.19.2; fastfetch 2.66.0</td><td>Coloured diffs; system information only when I run ff</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id="install">2. Install the tools, then the configuration</h2><p><strong>Version boundary:</strong> those are the versions installed when I inspected the setup on October 27, 2026. Homebrew installs its current releases; it does not reproduce that version list. For a matching-version baseline, use the corresponding official release tags linked below and keep the bundled Piper revision. The bundle reproduces the configuration, not an entire macOS image. I checked these files on my Mac; I have not tested the entire installation on a clean Mac.</p>\n<p>With <a href="https://brew.sh/">Homebrew</a> already installed:</p>\n<pre><code>brew install --cask kitty\nbrew install bat glow lsd yazi fzf zoxide git-delta fastfetch python</code></pre>\n<p>Install the four <a href="https://github.com/romkatv/powerlevel10k#fonts">MesloLGS NF font files</a>—Regular, Bold, Italic and Bold Italic—using Font Book. This is the font family my config names. A similarly named Nerd Font may need a different <code>font_family</code> value. You do not need to install the Powerlevel10k prompt to use its font.</p>\n<p>My Kitty app lives in <code>~/Applications/kitty.app</code>, and several tools live in <code>~/bin</code>. Homebrew will normally use different paths. The download contains two templates and a small preparation script that substitutes the paths returned by <code>command -v</code>. It writes only inside the extracted download, not into your live configuration:</p>\n<pre><code>cd &quot;$HOME/Downloads/kitty-setup&quot;\npython3 prepare.py\nless kitty/kitty.conf\nless yazi/yazi.toml</code></pre>\n<p>The bundle’s <code>README.md</code> has the complete backup and copy commands. Back up first, then copy the three Kitty files together. Merge the shell snippet into your existing setup rather than replace your entire <code>.zshrc</code>; it may contain unrelated configuration. The resulting layout is:</p>\n<pre><code>~/.config/kitty/\n  kitty.conf\n  theme.conf\n  tab_bar.py\n~/.config/terminal-setup/terminal.zsh\n~/.config/yazi/\n  yazi.toml\n  plugins/piper.yazi/main.lua\n  plugins/piper.yazi/LICENSE\n~/Library/Preferences/glow/glow.yml</code></pre>\n<p>Source the shell snippet once, near the end of <code>~/.zshrc</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>source &quot;$HOME/.config/terminal-setup/terminal.zsh&quot;</code></pre>\n<p>Ensure Homebrew is already on your shell’s <code>PATH</code>. My Apple Silicon login profile uses <code>eval "$(/opt/homebrew/bin/brew shellenv zsh)"</code>; Intel Macs usually have a different prefix. The guide assumes standard config locations, not a custom <code>ZDOTDIR</code> or <code>XDG_CONFIG_HOME</code>. Save work and open a fresh Kitty window after installing.</p>\n<h2 id="appearance">3. The appearance: one palette, several renderers</h2><p>My font settings are small enough to keep together:</p>\n<pre><code>font_family MesloLGS NF\nbold_font auto\nitalic_font auto\nbold_italic_font auto\nfont_size 14.0\ntext_composition_strategy 1.7 30\ndisable_ligatures never\nmacos_thicken_font 0.25</code></pre>\n<p>The two composition numbers are gamma and contrast adjustments, not font weights. Allowing ligatures does not add ligatures to a font that lacks them. These are appearance preferences, not measured performance improvements.</p>\n<p>The background is <code>#282c34</code>, text is <code>#abb2bf</code>, cyan is <code>#56b6c2</code>, blue is <code>#61afef</code>, green is <code>#98c379</code>, and red is <code>#e06c75</code>. The complete 16-colour palette is in <code>theme.conf</code>. Bat and delta use <code>TwoDark</code>; Glow uses its own dark rendering, so not every application shares identical colour tokens.</p>\n<pre><code>window_padding_width 10\nbackground_opacity 0.95\ndynamic_background_opacity yes\nbackground_blur 24\nhide_window_decorations titlebar-only\ninitial_window_width 120c\ninitial_window_height 34c\nremember_window_size yes</code></pre>\n<p>The initial dimensions are character cells. Once Kitty remembers a window size, that saved size can take precedence. The 95% opacity and blur mean the desktop behind the window also affects its appearance.</p>\n<pre><code>cursor_shape beam\ncursor_blink_interval 0.5\ncursor_trail 1\ncursor_trail_decay 0.1 0.4\nrepaint_delay 8\ninput_delay 2\nsync_to_monitor yes</code></pre>\n<p><code>cursor_trail 1</code> is a millisecond threshold, not a one-pixel trail. The decay values control its fade timing. The repaint and input delays are batching controls; they are not measured end-to-end typing latency, and monitor synchronisation still limits rendering. Lower values can cost CPU. Blur is another possible cost. See <a href="https://sw.kovidgoyal.net/kitty/conf/">Kitty’s configuration reference</a> for the actual semantics.</p>\n<h2 id="panes">4. Panes, tabs and the legend</h2><p>Kitty calls a pane a <em>window</em>; an OS window contains tabs, and each tab contains those panes. My enabled layouts are <code>splits,grid,stack,tall,fat</code>. I use native panes here, not tmux. They do not keep a remote job alive after a lost SSH connection.</p>\n<pre><code>map cmd+d       launch --location=vsplit --cwd=current\nmap cmd+shift+d launch --location=hsplit --cwd=current\nmap cmd+g       goto_layout grid\nmap cmd+shift+g goto_layout splits</code></pre>\n<div class="terminal-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Shortcut</th><th>Behaviour in my inspected configuration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>⌘D / ⇧⌘D</td><td>Side-by-side / stacked split, retaining the current working directory</td></tr><tr><td>⌘G / ⇧⌘G</td><td>Grid / manual splits layout</td></tr><tr><td>Ctrl+Shift+[ / ]</td><td>Previous / next pane</td></tr><tr><td>Ctrl+Shift+W</td><td>Close the focused pane</td></tr><tr><td>⌘W or Ctrl+Shift+Q</td><td>Close the tab, not just the focused pane</td></tr><tr><td>⌘T; ⇧⌘[ / ]</td><td>New tab; previous / next tab</td></tr><tr><td>Ctrl+Shift+L</td><td>Cycle enabled layouts, including stack</td></tr><tr><td>⌘F</td><td>Open scrollback search through the pager</td></tr><tr><td>Ctrl+Shift+Z / X</td><td>Previous / next prompt with shell integration</td></tr><tr><td>Ctrl+Shift+E</td><td>URL hints</td></tr><tr><td>Ctrl+Shift+F3</td><td>Command palette (Fn may be needed on a Mac keyboard)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>My <code>tab_bar.py</code> delegates tab drawing to Kitty’s powerline renderer, then appends a colour-coded legend after the last tab. Cyan means pane actions, gold means tabs, and red means closing. The bar is shown even with one tab.</p>\n<p><strong>A correction to my old notes:</strong> the legend’s Option+Command+arrow reminder is not backed by a mapping in this snapshot. Shift+Command+Enter is not mapped to zoom either. Use the verified shortcuts above. The legend also skips itself when there is insufficient space; it is not literally always visible. Its simple character counting is not a perfect terminal-cell-width calculation.</p>\n<p>If you want those extra bindings, add the following as an <em>optional change</em>. They are not part of my inspected configuration or the download:</p>\n<pre><code>map opt+cmd+left  neighboring_window left\nmap opt+cmd+right neighboring_window right\nmap opt+cmd+up    neighboring_window up\nmap opt+cmd+down  neighboring_window down\nmap cmd+shift+enter toggle_layout stack</code></pre>\n<h2 id="shell">5. The shell tools that make the theme useful</h2><p>The default macOS Zsh prompt is sufficient for this setup. The important changes are the commands behind it:</p>\n<pre><code>alias md=&#x27;glow -p&#x27;\nalias mdcat=&#x27;glow&#x27;\nexport GLAMOUR_STYLE=dark\nalias cat=&#x27;bat&#x27;\nexport BAT_THEME=&quot;TwoDark&quot;\nexport MANPAGER=&quot;sh -c &#x27;col -bx | bat -l man -p&#x27;&quot;\nexport MANROFFOPT=&quot;-c&quot;\nalias ls=&#x27;lsd --group-dirs first&#x27;\nalias ll=&#x27;lsd -l --group-dirs first&#x27;\nalias la=&#x27;lsd -la --group-dirs first&#x27;\nalias lt=&#x27;lsd --tree --depth 2&#x27;\nsource &lt;(fzf --zsh)\neval &quot;$(zoxide init zsh)&quot;\nalias ff=&#x27;fastfetch&#x27;</code></pre>\n<p>Use <code>command cat</code> or <code>\\cat</code> when you need plain output instead of bat. These are interactive aliases; scripts should call the program they actually need. <code>md</code> opens Glow’s pager, while <code>mdcat</code> prints Markdown directly. <code>ff</code> is optional and does not run at every shell startup.</p>\n<p><a href="https://github.com/junegunn/fzf#setting-up-shell-integration">fzf’s shell integration</a> supplies Ctrl+R for history, Ctrl+T for files and Alt+C for directory selection. The <code>--zsh</code> integration requires fzf 0.48 or newer. My left Option key acts as Alt. The full snippet also colours fzf to match the palette. <a href="https://github.com/ajeetdsouza/zoxide#installation">zoxide</a> learns directories as I visit them; a fresh installation will not know my navigation history.</p>\n<h3>Yazi and rendered Markdown</h3><p>⌘B launches Yazi in a side-by-side split using <code>--bias=22</code>. This requests a relatively narrow pane; its exact width depends on the active layout and available space. It is a normal terminal pane, not a special file-browser widget.</p><p>The missing dependency in many “copy my theme” guides is the previewer. My Yazi configuration routes <code>.md</code>, <code>.markdown</code> and <code>.mdx</code> files through <a href="https://github.com/yazi-rs/plugins/tree/main/piper.yazi">Piper</a>, which invokes Glow:</p>\n<pre><code>[[plugin.prepend_previewers]]\nurl = &quot;*.md&quot;\nrun = &#x27;piper -- CLICOLOR_FORCE=1 /absolute/path/to/glow -w=$w -s=dark &quot;$1&quot;&#x27;</code></pre>\n<p><code>$w</code> is the preview width and <code>$1</code> is the selected path. MDX is treated as Markdown here; Glow is not running React components. An opener rule also makes opening these files run <code>glow -p</code>. The complete download contains all three rules, the opener, and my installed Piper code with its MIT licence. It does not fetch a moving plugin branch during setup.</p>\n<p>Shift+Command+M uses Kitty path hints to open a selected on-screen path in a Glow overlay. It needs a real readable Markdown path; it is not an editor command. The preparation script resolves the executable path used by that mapping.</p>\n<h3>Git diffs</h3><p>I configure delta separately from Zsh:</p>\n<pre><code>git config --global core.pager delta\ngit config --global delta.navigate true\ngit config --global delta.line-numbers true\ngit config --global delta.syntax-theme TwoDark</code></pre>\n<p>These change your global Git pager settings, so inspect existing values first. They enable navigation, line numbers and the TwoDark syntax theme. My inspected config does not enable side-by-side mode. The relevant upstream reference is <a href="https://dandavison.github.io/delta/configuration.html">delta’s configuration guide</a>.</p>\n<h2 id="ssh">6. A colour cue for SSH</h2><p>My Zsh <code>ssh()</code> wrapper changes the background to <code>#1a1a2e</code>, text to <code>#f5f5f5</code>, and the cursor to <code>#ff5555</code> before calling the real SSH program. When it returns normally, the wrapper restores the three local colours and returns SSH’s exit status.</p>\n<pre><code>ssh() {\n  local local_bg=&quot;#282c34&quot; local_fg=&quot;#abb2bf&quot; local_cursor=&quot;#528bff&quot;\n  local ssh_bg=&quot;#1a1a2e&quot;   ssh_fg=&quot;#f5f5f5&quot;   ssh_cursor=&quot;#ff5555&quot;\n\n  printf &#x27;\\033]11;%s\\007&#x27; &quot;$ssh_bg&quot;\n  printf &#x27;\\033]10;%s\\007&#x27; &quot;$ssh_fg&quot;\n  printf &#x27;\\033]12;%s\\007&#x27; &quot;$ssh_cursor&quot;\n\n  TERM=xterm-256color command ssh &quot;$@&quot;\n  local ec=$?\n\n  printf &#x27;\\033]11;%s\\007&#x27; &quot;$local_bg&quot;\n  printf &#x27;\\033]10;%s\\007&#x27; &quot;$local_fg&quot;\n  printf &#x27;\\033]12;%s\\007&#x27; &quot;$local_cursor&quot;\n\n  return $ec\n}</code></pre>\n<p>OSC 10, 11 and 12 change text, background and cursor colours. <code>command ssh</code> prevents the wrapper calling itself. The <code>TERM=xterm-256color</code> fallback helps when a server lacks <code>xterm-kitty</code> terminfo, but it does not install remote aliases or force every remote program to use colour. <a href="https://sw.kovidgoyal.net/kitty/kittens/ssh/">Kitty’s SSH kitten</a> is another approach; it is not what this wrapper uses.</p>\n<p>This cue is deliberately limited. It changes colour even if authentication fails, does not prove which host I reached, and resets to hard-coded local colours rather than a saved stack. Nested sessions or an interrupted shell can leave an unexpected colour. It is a reminder, not a safety check. The wrapper is included so you can reproduce it, not because these edge cases are solved.</p>\n<h2 id="checks">7. Verify it before relying on it</h2><p>From a new Kitty shell, run:</p>\n<pre><code>command -v bat glow lsd yazi fzf zoxide delta fastfetch\nalias cat ls md\nwhence -w fzf-history-widget z\nbat --list-themes\ncd &quot;$HOME/Downloads/kitty-setup/examples&quot;\nll\ncat hello.cpp\nmd README.md</code></pre>\n<p>Then try ⌘D, Ctrl+Shift+W, ⌘B and a new tab. Test Ctrl+R with harmless commands rather than photograph real history. My font can be checked with Kitty’s <code>kitten choose-fonts</code>; verify the family and all four faces before changing anything.</p>\n<div class="terminal-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>What to check</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Boxes instead of icons</td><td>MesloLGS NF is installed and the family name matches; reopen Kitty after font installation.</td></tr><tr><td>⌘B or Markdown hints fail</td><td>Run prepare.py after the tools are installed; inspect the resolved absolute paths.</td></tr><tr><td>Markdown preview is blank</td><td>Piper files are present, Glow is executable, and Yazi/Piper versions are compatible.</td></tr><tr><td>Ctrl+R does nothing</td><td>fzf --zsh was sourced once in the interactive shell; another plugin may override bindings.</td></tr><tr><td>Legend missing</td><td>The renderer omits it if the remaining tab-bar width is too small.</td></tr><tr><td>New shell has a plain prompt</td><td>Expected: this setup has no prompt framework.</td></tr><tr><td>Remote colours stay changed</td><td>The wrapper may not have returned normally; start a new shell/window or restore the three local colours.</td></tr><tr><td>Everything closed with ⌘W</td><td>That shortcut closes a tab; use Ctrl+Shift+W for one pane.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3>Two settings worth choosing deliberately</h3><p>The inspected configuration uses <code>confirm_os_window_close 0</code>, which disables close confirmation, and <code>allow_remote_control yes</code> with <code>listen_on unix:/tmp/mykitty</code>. Remote control allows programs to operate Kitty, including sending text and reading window content; with this broad setting, terminal programs reached over SSH can also be a concern. These are real choices in the snapshot, not requirements for the colour theme or shortcuts.</p>\n<p>For a safer variant, change remote control to <code>no</code> and remove <code>listen_on</code>; set close confirmation to <code>-1</code> if you want Kitty to account for running commands. Review <a href="https://sw.kovidgoyal.net/kitty/remote-control/">Kitty’s remote-control permissions</a> before opting into the broad setting. Nothing in this guide changes my installed configuration automatically.</p>\n<p>I keep 50,000 scrollback lines and a 100 MB pager-history allowance. Useful for build output, but scrollback can also contain sensitive command output. The download contains no history, SSH configuration, host addresses or credentials.</p>\n<h2>What I checked, and what remains manual</h2><p>I checked the installed versions, read the files, parsed the configuration using the installed Kitty library, and compared effective shortcut mappings rather than trust the old README. I also checked the prepared bundle and the site build. That does not verify every interactive behaviour on a fresh Mac. Genuine terminal screenshots are not included in this version; the visual above is labelled as a diagram.</p>\n<p>The most useful part of this setup is knowing which layer owns a behaviour. Kitty owns the panes. Zsh owns the aliases. Glow owns Markdown rendering. Once those boundaries are clear, the configuration becomes much easier to reproduce and debug.</p>\n<h3>Official references and matching versions</h3><ul>\n<li><a href="https://github.com/kovidgoyal/kitty/releases/tag/v0.48.2">Kitty 0.48.2</a> \xb7 <a href="https://sw.kovidgoyal.net/kitty/conf/">configuration</a> \xb7 <a href="https://sw.kovidgoyal.net/kitty/kittens/hints/">hints</a></li>\n<li><a href="https://github.com/romkatv/powerlevel10k#fonts">MesloLGS NF font files</a></li>\n<li><a href="https://github.com/sharkdp/bat/releases/tag/v0.26.1">bat 0.26.1</a> \xb7 <a href="https://github.com/charmbracelet/glow/releases/tag/v2.1.2">Glow 2.1.2</a> \xb7 <a href="https://github.com/lsd-rs/lsd/releases/tag/v1.2.0">lsd 1.2.0</a></li>\n<li><a href="https://github.com/sxyazi/yazi/releases/tag/v26.5.6">Yazi 26.5.6</a> \xb7 <a href="https://github.com/yazi-rs/plugins/tree/9014ed2/piper.yazi">Piper snapshot</a></li>\n<li><a href="https://github.com/junegunn/fzf/releases/tag/v0.74.2">fzf 0.74.2</a> \xb7 <a href="https://github.com/ajeetdsouza/zoxide/releases/tag/v0.10.0">zoxide 0.10.0</a></li>\n<li><a href="https://github.com/dandavison/delta/releases/tag/0.19.2">delta 0.19.2</a> \xb7 <a href="https://github.com/fastfetch-cli/fastfetch/releases/tag/2.66.0">fastfetch 2.66.0</a></li>\n</ul></div>'},{slug:"velocita-replicated-trading-on-a-raspberry-pi",title:"Velocita: Building a Replicated Trading System on Home Hardware",date:"2026-10-27",description:"Raft over Aeron on two NUCs and a Pi 5, 16 real swaps on Solana, a ledger that matched the chain to the billionth of a SOL, and every problem I had to solve to get there.",tags:["Distributed Systems","Raft","C++20","Aeron","Solana","Trading","Architecture"],githubUrl:"https://github.com/vishaljha2121/Velocita",content:'\n<p>\n    On October 15, a Raspberry Pi on my desk voted to buy $12.69 of SOL. Three seconds later the trade was on the Solana blockchain. Over the next three hours, with nobody touching anything, its cluster settled <strong>16 real swaps</strong>, and every one matched the blockchain <strong>to the billionth of a SOL</strong>.\n</p>\n\n<p>\n    It lost 95 cents. It also stopped on an order whose outcome it could not verify. The run tested execution and accounting; it did not demonstrate a profitable strategy.\n</p>\n\n<div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(170px, 1fr)); gap: 1rem; margin: 2.5rem 0;">\n    <div style="border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 8px; padding: 1rem 1.1rem; background: rgba(255,255,255,0.6);"><div style="font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: 2rem; line-height: 1.1;">16 / 16</div><div style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--secondary); margin-top: 0.4rem;">settlements equal to the chain</div></div>\n    <div style="border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 8px; padding: 1rem 1.1rem; background: rgba(255,255,255,0.6);"><div style="font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: 2rem; line-height: 1.1;">126 ms</div><div style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--secondary); margin-top: 0.4rem;">p50 commit across 3 machines</div></div>\n    <div style="border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 8px; padding: 1rem 1.1rem; background: rgba(255,255,255,0.6);"><div style="font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: 2rem; line-height: 1.1;">2 s</div><div style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--secondary); margin-top: 0.4rem;">intent to Solana block (p50)</div></div>\n    <div style="border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 8px; padding: 1rem 1.1rem; background: rgba(255,255,255,0.6);"><div style="font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: 2rem; line-height: 1.1;">125 ns</div><div style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--secondary); margin-top: 0.4rem;">reducer p50 \xb7 Apple M5 Pro microbenchmark</div></div>\n    <div style="border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 8px; padding: 1rem 1.1rem; background: rgba(255,255,255,0.6);"><div style="font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: 2rem; line-height: 1.1;">4–7 s</div><div style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--secondary); margin-top: 0.4rem;">graceful restart/rejoin \xb7 trading halted</div></div>\n    <div style="border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 8px; padding: 1rem 1.1rem; background: rgba(255,255,255,0.6);"><div style="font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: 2rem; line-height: 1.1; color: #16a34a;">0</div><div style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--secondary); margin-top: 0.4rem;">duplicate orders or unexplained balances</div></div>\n</div>\n\n<p style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--secondary);">Measurements cover different scopes: 25 applied live intents for commit latency, 16 successful swaps for block inclusion, and an in-memory reducer benchmark on an Apple M5 Pro (125 ns p50, 167 ns p99; no Raft, WAL or network). The 4–7 s restart times came from a halted-cluster rolling upgrade. <a href="https://github.com/vishaljha2121/Velocita/tree/main/archive/evidence/benchmarks_2026-10-27">Benchmark records</a> \xb7 <a href="https://github.com/vishaljha2121/Velocita/blob/main/archive/evidence/automated_launch_2026-10-15/effect25-recovery/rolling/README.md">restart record</a>.</p>\n\n<h2 style="margin-top: 3rem">1. The problem</h2>\n\n<p>An automated trading system has to handle failures beyond the signal itself:</p>\n\n<div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(220px, 1fr)); gap: 1rem; margin: 1.5rem 0 2rem;">\n    <div style="border-left: 3px solid #ef4444; padding: 0.5rem 1rem;"><strong>Crashes mid-send</strong><div style="color: var(--secondary); font-size: 0.95rem;">Restart must neither lose the order nor send it twice.</div></div>\n    <div style="border-left: 3px solid #ef4444; padding: 0.5rem 1rem;"><strong>Ambiguous venue responses</strong><div style="color: var(--secondary); font-size: 0.95rem;">"Failed" doesn\'t always mean "didn\'t happen".</div></div>\n    <div style="border-left: 3px solid #ef4444; padding: 0.5rem 1rem;"><strong>Limits outside the strategy</strong><div style="color: var(--secondary); font-size: 0.95rem;">The kernel must enforce spending and loss limits even if signal code is wrong.</div></div>\n    <div style="border-left: 3px solid #ef4444; padding: 0.5rem 1rem;"><strong>Cheap hardware</strong><div style="color: var(--secondary); font-size: 0.95rem;">Two mini PCs and a Pi on home Wi-Fi.</div></div>\n</div>\n\n<p>I wanted to test these failure boundaries with a real $25 account and hardware I could operate at home.</p>\n\n<h2 style="margin-top: 3rem">2. The idea: separate deciding from doing</h2>\n\n<p>Money and decisions live in one deterministic state machine that three machines replicate with Raft. Everything that touches the outside world runs separately, acts only on committed state, and brings back evidence.</p>\n\n<div class="mermaid" style="display: flex; justify-content: center; margin-bottom: 2rem; background: transparent;">\nflowchart LR\n  subgraph NUC1["NUC1 \xb7 x86_64"]\n    V1["voter 1"]\n    DRV["strategy"]\n    GW["gateway"]\n    SG["signer"]\n  end\n  subgraph PI["Raspberry Pi 5"]\n    V2["voter 2"]\n  end\n  subgraph NUC2["NUC2 \xb7 x86_64"]\n    V3["voter 3"]\n  end\n  V1 <-->|"Raft over Aeron UDP"| V2\n  V2 <--> V3\n  V1 <--> V3\n  DRV -->|"intent"| V1\n  GW <-->|"state, evidence"| V1\n  GW <--> SG\n  GW <--> TM(["exchange"])\n  TM --> SOL(["Solana"])\n  GW -->|"finalized reads"| SOL\n</div>\n\n<p>The strategy proposes; the kernel, gateway and signer each enforce a different part of the execution contract:</p>\n\n<div class="mermaid" style="display: flex; justify-content: center; margin-bottom: 2rem; background: transparent;">\nsequenceDiagram\n  participant S as Strategy\n  participant R as Raft (3 voters)\n  participant G as Gateway\n  participant K as Signer\n  participant T as True Markets\n  participant C as Solana\n  S->>R: propose a buy\n  R->>R: commit on 2 of 3, reserve cash\n  G->>R: read the committed intent\n  G->>T: create unsigned swap\n  T-->>G: exact payload\n  G->>R: Prepare (payload digest)\n  G->>K: sign this exact transaction\n  K->>R: is this what was committed?\n  K-->>G: signature\n  G->>R: Attempt\n  G->>T: execute\n  T->>C: submit transaction\n  G->>C: read finalized transaction\n  G->>R: settlement with exact amounts\n</div>\n\n<table style="width: 100%; text-align: left; border-collapse: collapse; margin: 2rem 0; font-size: 0.95rem;">\n    <thead>\n        <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">\n            <th style="padding: 0.75rem 0;">Decision</th>\n            <th style="padding: 0.75rem 0;">Intuition</th>\n        </tr>\n    </thead>\n    <tbody>\n        <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);"><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem 0.75rem 0; vertical-align: top;"><strong>ADR 0005</strong> kernel behind Raft</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 0; color: var(--secondary);">Same pure function + same log = same cents on every machine. No clocks, floats or heap.</td></tr>\n        <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);"><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem 0.75rem 0; vertical-align: top;"><strong>ADR 0020</strong> callers have roles</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 0; color: var(--secondary);">The strategy can ask, never authorize. Even the operator can\'t report a fill.</td></tr>\n        <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);"><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem 0.75rem 0; vertical-align: top;"><strong>ADR 0021</strong> integer money</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 0; color: var(--secondary);">Lamports and USDC micros. The $10 loss stop lives in replicated state.</td></tr>\n        <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);"><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem 0.75rem 0; vertical-align: top;"><strong>Gateway journals</strong> before external acts</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 0; color: var(--secondary);">Write "about to send" before sending, so a crash means "maybe sent", never "send again".</td></tr>\n        <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);"><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem 0.75rem 0; vertical-align: top;"><strong>ADR 0019</strong> one key holder</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 0; color: var(--secondary);">Only the signer holds the key, and it checks the exact transaction itself.</td></tr>\n        <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);"><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem 0.75rem 0; vertical-align: top;"><strong>ADR 0025</strong> chain is the truth</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 0; color: var(--secondary);">The ledger books settlement only after finalized evidence matches the signed transaction.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h2 style="margin-top: 3rem">3. The SD card that couldn\'t keep up</h2>\n\n<div style="margin: 2rem 0;">\n    <div style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--secondary); margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Commit latency, p50 \xb7 different hardware and workloads</div>\n    <div style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.75rem; margin-bottom: 0.4rem;"><div style="width: 9rem; font-size: 0.85rem;">V1.1 \xb7 one Pi</div><div style="flex: 1; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.05); border-radius: 4px;"><div style="width: 100%; background: #ef4444; height: 22px; border-radius: 4px;"></div></div><div style="width: 4.5rem; font-size: 0.9rem; font-weight: 600;">396 ms</div></div>\n    <div style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.75rem;"><div style="width: 9rem; font-size: 0.85rem;">V2 \xb7 live</div><div style="flex: 1; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.05); border-radius: 4px;"><div style="width: 32%; background: #22c55e; height: 22px; border-radius: 4px;"></div></div><div style="width: 4.5rem; font-size: 0.9rem; font-weight: 600;">126 ms</div></div>\n</div>\n\n<p>The chart compares V1.1 at 5 commands/s on one Pi with 25 sparse live intents across three machines. It shows the project’s history, not a controlled speedup from one code change.</p>\n\n<p>Three voters on one Pi did ~9 <code>fsync</code>s per command through one SD card, and the Raft thread waited on every one. At 20 commands/s the cluster lost its leader.</p>\n\n<p>The WAL change moved writes and syncs to a writer thread. The Raft thread hands it sealed 4 KiB blocks over a lock-free ring; it can still face backpressure when the arena fills.</p>\n\n<pre style="background: #ffffff; color: var(--foreground); border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 1.25rem 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px; overflow-x: auto; font-family: monospace; font-size: 0.9rem; margin-bottom: 2rem">bool push(const T&amp; value) noexcept {\n    const auto head = head_.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);\n    const auto tail = tail_.load(std::memory_order_acquire);\n    if (head - tail &gt;= Capacity) return false;      // full: refuse, never overwrite\n    slots_[head &amp; (Capacity - 1)] = value;\n    head_.store(head + 1, std::memory_order_release); // publish\n    return true;\n}</pre>\n\n<div style="margin: 2rem 0;">\n    <div style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--secondary); margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">WAL owner-loop microbenchmark \xb7 Apple M5 Pro \xb7 median</div>\n    <div style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.75rem; margin-bottom: 0.4rem;"><div style="width: 9rem; font-size: 0.85rem;">fsync on thread</div><div style="flex: 1; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.05); border-radius: 4px;"><div style="width: 100%; background: #ef4444; height: 22px; border-radius: 4px;"></div></div><div style="width: 4.5rem; font-size: 0.9rem; font-weight: 600;">20.6 \xb5s</div></div>\n    <div style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.75rem;"><div style="width: 9rem; font-size: 0.85rem;">writer thread</div><div style="flex: 1; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.05); border-radius: 4px;"><div style="width: 0.6%; min-width: 3px; background: #22c55e; height: 22px; border-radius: 4px;"></div></div><div style="width: 4.5rem; font-size: 0.9rem; font-weight: 600;">42 ns</div></div>\n    <div style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: #16a34a; margin-top: 0.5rem; font-weight: 600;">490\xd7 lower median owner-loop time; not durable-commit latency</div>\n</div>\n\n<p>The October 27 storage run measured 20.584 \xb5s inline versus 0.042 \xb5s off-thread. The off-thread time covers staging and submission, including arena backpressure, rather than waiting for the record to become durable. Its p99 was 160.166 \xb5s versus 111.375 \xb5s inline: the lower median did not remove tail latency. <a href="https://github.com/vishaljha2121/Velocita/blob/main/archive/evidence/benchmarks_2026-10-27/v2-storage-benchmark.txt">Raw measurements</a>.</p>\n\n<h3>Why asynchronous storage needed a Raft change</h3>\n\n<p>The upstream library assumes synchronous persistence and counts the leader\'s own vote when an entry is appended. An asynchronous integration must change that assumption. This hypothetical failure shows why; it was not an observed loss in Velocita:</p>\n\n<div class="mermaid" style="display: flex; justify-content: center; margin-bottom: 2rem; background: transparent;">\nsequenceDiagram\n  participant L as Leader\n  participant F1 as Follower 1\n  participant F2 as Follower 2\n  L->>F1: entry 42 (leader\'s copy only in memory)\n  F1-->>L: durable\n  Note over L: majority, commit 42\n  Note over L: crash, unsafe integration restarts without 42\n  Note over F1: briefly unreachable\n  L->>F2: elect a leader: neither has 42\n  Note over L,F2: committed entry 42 is gone\n</div>\n\n<div style="border-left: 3px solid var(--foreground); padding: 0.75rem 1.25rem; margin: 2rem 0; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.03);">\n    <div style="font-size: 0.8rem; letter-spacing: 0.08em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--secondary); margin-bottom: 0.25rem;">ADR 0006 \xb7 the fix</div>\n    <div>A pinned fork adds a <code>durable_idx</code> hook: the leader\'s vote counts only up to what is really on disk. The build refuses to compile without the patch.</div>\n</div>\n\n<p>This example assumes the restarted node is allowed to serve despite losing its volatile suffix. Current Velocita separately refuses recovery when a persisted commit index exceeds the recovered log. The durable-quorum patch prevents the unsafe acknowledgement in the first place.</p>\n\n<h2 style="margin-top: 3rem">4. Fixed budgets for kernel state</h2>\n\n<p>The deterministic reducer uses fixed-size tables, and the retained log and WAL use startup-allocated arenas. Full tables refuse admission; a full WAL arena creates backpressure. The surrounding node still allocates for proposal payloads, peer messages and persistence jobs, so the whole Raft path is not allocation-free.</p>\n\n<div style="margin: 2rem 0;">\n    <div style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--secondary); margin-bottom: 0.6rem;">Size budgets, enforced by <code>static_assert</code>: green under 90%, amber 90–99%, dark exactly at budget (deliberate: one cache line, the swap account sized to fit)</div>\n    <div style="margin-bottom: 0.6rem;"><div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; gap: 1rem; font-size: 0.85rem;"><span>whole replicated state</span><span style="font-weight: 600; white-space: nowrap;">78,512 / 81,920 B</span></div><div style="background: rgba(0,0,0,0.06); border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 0.2rem;"><div style="width: 95.8%; background: #f59e0b; height: 14px; border-radius: 4px;"></div></div></div><div style="margin-bottom: 0.6rem;"><div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; gap: 1rem; font-size: 0.85rem;"><span>canonical snapshot</span><span style="font-weight: 600; white-space: nowrap;">61,586 / 65,536 B</span></div><div style="background: rgba(0,0,0,0.06); border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 0.2rem;"><div style="width: 94.0%; background: #f59e0b; height: 14px; border-radius: 4px;"></div></div></div><div style="margin-bottom: 0.6rem;"><div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; gap: 1rem; font-size: 0.85rem;"><span>swap account</span><span style="font-weight: 600; white-space: nowrap;">4,160 / 4,160 B</span></div><div style="background: rgba(0,0,0,0.06); border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 0.2rem;"><div style="width: 100.0%; background: var(--foreground); height: 14px; border-radius: 4px;"></div></div></div><div style="margin-bottom: 0.6rem;"><div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; gap: 1rem; font-size: 0.85rem;"><span>kernel result</span><span style="font-weight: 600; white-space: nowrap;">112 / 128 B</span></div><div style="background: rgba(0,0,0,0.06); border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 0.2rem;"><div style="width: 87.5%; background: #22c55e; height: 14px; border-radius: 4px;"></div></div></div><div style="margin-bottom: 0.6rem;"><div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; gap: 1rem; font-size: 0.85rem;"><span>swap command</span><span style="font-weight: 600; white-space: nowrap;">296 / 512 B</span></div><div style="background: rgba(0,0,0,0.06); border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 0.2rem;"><div style="width: 57.8%; background: #22c55e; height: 14px; border-radius: 4px;"></div></div></div><div style="margin-bottom: 0.6rem;"><div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; gap: 1rem; font-size: 0.85rem;"><span>hot record (one cache line)</span><span style="font-weight: 600; white-space: nowrap;">64 / 64 B</span></div><div style="background: rgba(0,0,0,0.06); border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 0.2rem;"><div style="width: 100.0%; background: var(--foreground); height: 14px; border-radius: 4px;"></div></div></div>\n</div>\n\n<pre style="background: #ffffff; color: var(--foreground); border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 1.25rem 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px; overflow-x: auto; font-family: monospace; font-size: 0.9rem; margin-bottom: 2rem">static_assert(std::is_trivially_copyable_v&lt;State&gt;);\nstatic_assert(sizeof(State)   &lt;= 4160);   // add a field, the build fails\nstatic_assert(sizeof(Command) &lt;= 512);</pre>\n\n<div style="margin: 2rem 0;">\n    <div style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--secondary); margin-bottom: 0.6rem;">The memory atlas: where the 80 KiB state budget goes</div>\n    <div style="display: flex; height: 30px; border-radius: 4px; overflow: hidden;"><div title="request receipts: 40,960 B" style="width: 50.00%; background: #6366f1; "></div><div title="decision records: 10,368 B" style="width: 12.66%; background: #8b5cf6; "></div><div title="policies: 7,360 B" style="width: 8.98%; background: #0ea5e9; "></div><div title="source receipts: 7,168 B" style="width: 8.75%; background: #14b8a6; "></div><div title="policy bundles: 4,176 B" style="width: 5.10%; background: #22c55e; "></div><div title="swap account: 4,160 B" style="width: 5.08%; background: #f59e0b; "></div><div title="risk, orders, cursors: 4,320 B" style="width: 5.27%; background: #94a3b8; "></div><div title="free: 3,408 B" style="width: 4.16%; background: transparent; border: 1px dashed var(--border);"></div></div>\n    <div style="margin-top: 0.6rem; line-height: 1.8;"><span style="display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.35rem; margin-right: 1rem; font-size: 0.8rem; color: var(--secondary);"><span style="width: 10px; height: 10px; border-radius: 2px; background: #6366f1;  display: inline-block;"></span>request receipts 40.0 KiB</span><span style="display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.35rem; margin-right: 1rem; font-size: 0.8rem; color: var(--secondary);"><span style="width: 10px; height: 10px; border-radius: 2px; background: #8b5cf6;  display: inline-block;"></span>decision records 10.1 KiB</span><span style="display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.35rem; margin-right: 1rem; font-size: 0.8rem; color: var(--secondary);"><span style="width: 10px; height: 10px; border-radius: 2px; background: #0ea5e9;  display: inline-block;"></span>policies 7.2 KiB</span><span style="display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.35rem; margin-right: 1rem; font-size: 0.8rem; color: var(--secondary);"><span style="width: 10px; height: 10px; border-radius: 2px; background: #14b8a6;  display: inline-block;"></span>source receipts 7.0 KiB</span><span style="display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.35rem; margin-right: 1rem; font-size: 0.8rem; color: var(--secondary);"><span style="width: 10px; height: 10px; border-radius: 2px; background: #22c55e;  display: inline-block;"></span>policy bundles 4.1 KiB</span><span style="display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.35rem; margin-right: 1rem; font-size: 0.8rem; color: var(--secondary);"><span style="width: 10px; height: 10px; border-radius: 2px; background: #f59e0b;  display: inline-block;"></span>swap account 4.1 KiB</span><span style="display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.35rem; margin-right: 1rem; font-size: 0.8rem; color: var(--secondary);"><span style="width: 10px; height: 10px; border-radius: 2px; background: #94a3b8;  display: inline-block;"></span>risk, orders, cursors 4.2 KiB</span><span style="display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.35rem; margin-right: 1rem; font-size: 0.8rem; color: var(--secondary);"><span style="width: 10px; height: 10px; border-radius: 2px; background: transparent; border: 1px dashed var(--secondary); display: inline-block;"></span>free 3.3 KiB</span></div>\n</div>\n\n<p>Half the state is receipts: the stored answer to each recent command, so a retry gets the original answer instead of a second trade.</p>\n\n<table style="width: 100%; text-align: left; border-collapse: collapse; margin: 2rem 0; font-size: 0.95rem;">\n    <thead>\n        <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);"><th style="padding: 0.75rem 0;">Arena</th><th style="padding: 0.75rem 0;">Slots</th><th style="padding: 0.75rem 0; text-align: right;">Total</th><th style="padding: 0.75rem 0 0.75rem 1rem;">When full</th></tr>\n    </thead>\n    <tbody>\n        <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);"><td style="padding: 0.75rem 0;">retained Raft log</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 0;">8,192 \xd7 2 KiB ring</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 0; text-align: right;">16 MiB</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 0 0.75rem 1rem; color: var(--secondary);">refuse until compaction (64 cells kept for "halt")</td></tr>\n        <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);"><td style="padding: 0.75rem 0;">WAL blocks</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 0;">64 \xd7 4 KiB</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 0; text-align: right;">256 KiB</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 0 0.75rem 1rem; color: var(--secondary);">backpressure</td></tr>\n        <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);"><td style="padding: 0.75rem 0;">result ledger</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 0;">1,024 answers</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 0; text-align: right;">–</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 0 0.75rem 1rem; color: var(--secondary);">refuse new commands, never drop an answer</td></tr>\n        <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);"><td style="padding: 0.75rem 0;">state tables</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 0;">fixed arrays + count</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 0; text-align: right;">77 KiB</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 0 0.75rem 1rem; color: var(--secondary);">typed "capacity exceeded"</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<div style="margin: 2rem 0;">\n    <div style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--secondary); margin-bottom: 0.6rem;">The 64 WAL blocks at one illustrative moment: the Raft thread fills, the writer thread syncs</div>\n    <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(16, 1fr); gap: 3px; max-width: 520px;"><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: #22c55e;"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: #22c55e;"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: #22c55e;"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: #22c55e;"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: #22c55e;"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: #22c55e;"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: #22c55e;"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: #22c55e;"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: #22c55e;"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: #22c55e;"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: #22c55e;"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: #22c55e;"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: #22c55e;"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: #22c55e;"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: #22c55e;"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: #22c55e;"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: #22c55e;"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: #22c55e;"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: #22c55e;"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: #22c55e;"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: #22c55e;"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: #22c55e;"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: #f59e0b;"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: #f59e0b;"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: #f59e0b;"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: #f59e0b;"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: #f59e0b;"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: #6366f1;"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.08);"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.08);"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.08);"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.08);"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.08);"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.08);"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.08);"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.08);"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.08);"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.08);"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.08);"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.08);"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.08);"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.08);"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.08);"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.08);"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.08);"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.08);"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.08);"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.08);"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.08);"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.08);"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.08);"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.08);"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.08);"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.08);"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.08);"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.08);"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.08);"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.08);"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.08);"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.08);"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.08);"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.08);"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.08);"></div><div style="aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 2px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.08);"></div></div>\n    <div style="margin-top: 0.6rem; font-size: 0.8rem; color: var(--secondary);"><span style="color: #22c55e;">■</span> durable, reusable &nbsp; <span style="color: #f59e0b;">■</span> sealed, waiting for fsync &nbsp; <span style="color: #6366f1;">■</span> open, being filled &nbsp; ■ never used</div>\n</div>\n\n<div style="border-left: 3px solid var(--foreground); padding: 0.75rem 1.25rem; margin: 2rem 0; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.03);">\n    <div style="font-size: 0.8rem; letter-spacing: 0.08em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--secondary); margin-bottom: 0.25rem;">ADR 0022 \xb7 budgets are compile errors</div>\n    <div>The snapshot must fit one 64 KiB message. As a runtime check, growing a struct would surface in production as a snapshot that no longer fits; as a <code>static_assert</code>, the build fails on both CPUs and whoever adds the field decides what to give up.</div>\n</div>\n\n<h2 style="margin-top: 3rem">5. Elections nobody asked for</h2>\n\n<div style="margin: 2rem 0;">\n    <div style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--secondary); margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Compaction pause (election timeout is 1–2 s)</div>\n    <div style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.75rem; margin-bottom: 0.4rem;"><div style="width: 9rem; font-size: 0.85rem;">re-read everything</div><div style="flex: 1; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.05); border-radius: 4px;"><div style="width: 100%; background: #ef4444; height: 22px; border-radius: 4px;"></div></div><div style="width: 4.5rem; font-size: 0.9rem; font-weight: 600;">4.8 s</div></div>\n    <div style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.75rem;"><div style="width: 9rem; font-size: 0.85rem;">rotating scrub</div><div style="flex: 1; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.05); border-radius: 4px;"><div style="width: 8%; background: #22c55e; height: 22px; border-radius: 4px;"></div></div><div style="width: 4.5rem; font-size: 0.9rem; font-weight: 600;">≤ 0.4 s</div></div>\n</div>\n\n<table style="width: 100%; text-align: left; border-collapse: collapse; margin: 2rem 0; font-size: 0.95rem;">\n    <thead>\n        <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);"><th style="padding: 0.75rem 0;">Symptom</th><th style="padding: 0.75rem 0;">Cause</th><th style="padding: 0.75rem 0;">Fix</th></tr>\n    </thead>\n    <tbody>\n        <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);"><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem 0.75rem 0; color: #ef4444;"><strong>term 1 → 65</strong> in 48 h</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem 0.75rem 0;">every compaction re-read all archives</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 0;">check 2 segments per compaction, in rotation</td></tr>\n        <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);"><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem 0.75rem 0; color: #ef4444;"><strong>term 49 → 191</strong> in 6 h</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem 0.75rem 0;">a voter back from a Wi-Fi drop deposed the leader</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 0;">pre-vote: ask before campaigning</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h2 style="margin-top: 3rem">6. The gateway that stopped itself, twice</h2>\n\n<p>The gateway stops when it cannot safely account for an operation. Two bugs showed where that behavior was too broad:</p>\n\n<table style="width: 100%; text-align: left; border-collapse: collapse; margin: 2rem 0; font-size: 0.95rem;">\n    <thead>\n        <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);"><th style="padding: 0.75rem 0;">When</th><th style="padding: 0.75rem 0;">What happened</th><th style="padding: 0.75rem 0;">Fix</th></tr>\n    </thead>\n    <tbody>\n        <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);"><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem 0.75rem 0; vertical-align: top;">Oct 9, paper</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem 0.75rem 0;">Heard "filled" from a follower, then read older state from a slower leader (the Pi)</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 0;">read fence: discard any state older than what you\'ve seen</td></tr>\n        <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);"><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem 0.75rem 0; vertical-align: top;">Oct 15, pre-live</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem 0.75rem 0;">One failed read during an election stopped it for good</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 0;">retry reads for 120 s; failed <em>commands</em> still stop</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h2 style="margin-top: 3rem">7. A separate signer checks the transaction</h2>\n\n<p>The gateway talks to the internet, so it never sees the key. The signer re-checks everything itself before signing:</p>\n\n<div class="mermaid" style="display: flex; justify-content: center; margin-bottom: 2rem; background: transparent;">\nflowchart LR\n  A["sign request"] --> B["re-read Raft:<br/>effect prepared?"]\n  B --> C["decode the transaction:<br/>amounts, fee, DEX match?"]\n  C --> D["authority proof<br/>from Raft"]\n  D --> E["journal, then sign"]\n</div>\n\n<p>And here is how every secret travels, and who ends up holding what:</p>\n\n<div class="mermaid" style="display: flex; justify-content: center; margin-bottom: 2rem; background: transparent;">\nflowchart LR\n  KC["Mac Keychain<br/>(root of trust)"] -->|"SSH stdin, never a file<br/>or command line"| TF["each host: RAM-only tmpfs<br/>dir 0700, file 0600"]\n  TF -->|"read-only mount<br/>of one file"| P["voters, driver, gateway<br/>32-byte HMAC caller keys"]\n  VK["exchange API key<br/>(downloaded by me)"] -->|"owner-only install"| SG["signer, own Unix user<br/>network: 3 voters only"]\n  SG -->|"signs a 47-byte<br/>uuid.timestamp only"| GW["gateway gets a<br/>short-lived token"]\n  SG -->|"signs only the<br/>committed transaction"| TX["swap signature"]\n</div>\n\n<table style="width: 100%; text-align: left; border-collapse: collapse; margin: 2rem 0; font-size: 0.95rem;">\n    <thead>\n        <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);"><th style="padding: 0.75rem 0;">Process</th><th style="padding: 0.75rem 0;">Holds</th><th style="padding: 0.75rem 0;">Can\'t</th></tr>\n    </thead>\n    <tbody>\n        <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);"><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem 0.75rem 0;">strategy</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem 0.75rem 0;">its caller key</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 0; color: var(--secondary);">authorize, sign, or report a fill</td></tr>\n        <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);"><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem 0.75rem 0;">gateway</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem 0.75rem 0;">its caller key, a session token</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 0; color: var(--secondary);">sign anything the cluster didn\'t commit</td></tr>\n        <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);"><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem 0.75rem 0;">signer</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem 0.75rem 0;">the exchange key</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 0; color: var(--secondary);">reach the internet, or sign arbitrary bytes</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<p>Every message is tagged with a key derived per direction and per cluster, so a captured frame can\'t be replayed backwards or into another cluster. Deployed service credentials are placed in tmpfs, rather than the voter’s SD-card data directory. The owner’s Keychain and downloaded API-key file are separate custody responsibilities.</p>\n\n<div style="border-left: 3px solid var(--foreground); padding: 0.75rem 1.25rem; margin: 2rem 0; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.03);">\n    <div style="font-size: 0.8rem; letter-spacing: 0.08em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--secondary); margin-bottom: 0.25rem;">ADR 0019 \xb7 decode, don\'t simulate</div>\n    <div>A simulation shows what a transaction would do <em>now</em>. Decoding proves what the signature <em>allows</em>: which accounts, how much at most, and the minimum that must arrive.</div>\n</div>\n\n<h2 style="margin-top: 3rem">8. 16 out of 16, to the atom</h2>\n\n<p>Every amount in Velocita is an integer count of an asset\'s smallest unit, called an <strong>atom</strong>:</p>\n\n<div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(240px, 1fr)); gap: 1rem; margin: 1.5rem 0 2rem;">\n    <div style="border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 8px; padding: 1rem 1.1rem; background: rgba(255,255,255,0.6);"><div style="font-size: 0.8rem; letter-spacing: 0.08em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--secondary);">SOL \xb7 base</div><div style="font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: 1.5rem; line-height: 1.2; margin-top: 0.3rem;">1 SOL = 1,000,000,000 lamports</div><div style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--secondary); margin-top: 0.3rem;"><code>BaseAtoms</code></div></div>\n    <div style="border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 8px; padding: 1rem 1.1rem; background: rgba(255,255,255,0.6);"><div style="font-size: 0.8rem; letter-spacing: 0.08em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--secondary);">USDC \xb7 quote</div><div style="font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: 1.5rem; line-height: 1.2; margin-top: 0.3rem;">1 USDC = 1,000,000 micros</div><div style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--secondary); margin-top: 0.3rem;"><code>QuoteAtoms</code></div></div>\n</div>\n\n<div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(200px, 1fr)); gap: 1rem; margin: 0 0 2rem;">\n    <div style="border-left: 3px solid #22c55e; padding: 0.5rem 1rem;"><strong>Same bits everywhere</strong><div style="color: var(--secondary); font-size: 0.95rem;">Integer atoms with explicit overflow and rounding rules avoid floating-point variation in accounting across CPUs.</div></div>\n    <div style="border-left: 3px solid #22c55e; padding: 0.5rem 1rem;"><strong>Equal to the chain</strong><div style="color: var(--secondary); font-size: 0.95rem;">Solana stores balances in these units, so a settlement compares with <code>==</code>, no tolerance.</div></div>\n    <div style="border-left: 3px solid #22c55e; padding: 0.5rem 1rem;"><strong>Can\'t be mixed up</strong><div style="color: var(--secondary); font-size: 0.95rem;">Two distinct types: adding lamports to USDC doesn\'t compile.</div></div>\n</div>\n\n<pre style="background: #ffffff; color: var(--foreground); border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 1.25rem 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px; overflow-x: auto; font-family: monospace; font-size: 0.9rem; margin-bottom: 2rem">using BaseAtoms  = StrongId&lt;BaseAtomTag&gt;;    // lamports\nusing QuoteAtoms = StrongId&lt;QuoteAtomTag&gt;;   // USDC micros</pre>\n\n<p>One buy, followed through the system in atoms:</p>\n\n<div class="mermaid" style="display: flex; justify-content: center; margin-bottom: 2rem; background: transparent;">\nflowchart LR\n  D["strategy<br/>debit 12,690,000<br/>min 110,400,000 lamports"] --> K["kernel<br/>reserves 12,690,000"]\n  K --> S["signer<br/>fee = 12,690,000 \xf7 10,000 \xd7 20<br/>= 25,380 exactly"]\n  S --> C["Solana<br/>+119,507,269 lamports<br/>−12,690,000 micros"]\n  C --> T["settlement<br/>same integers"]\n  T --> UI["dashboard<br/>$ only for display"]\n</div>\n\n<p>That division is why every buy is a whole number of cents: the signer refuses an input that isn\'t a multiple of 10,000 atoms, so the fee always comes out exact. And the books and the chain end up holding the same integers:</p>\n\n<table style="width: 100%; text-align: left; border-collapse: collapse; margin: 2rem 0; font-size: 0.95rem;">\n    <thead>\n        <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);"><th style="padding: 0.75rem 0;">Effect 1 (buy)</th><th style="padding: 0.75rem 0; text-align: right;">In Raft</th><th style="padding: 0.75rem 0; text-align: right;">On chain</th></tr>\n    </thead>\n    <tbody>\n        <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);"><td style="padding: 0.75rem 0;">SOL (lamports)</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 0; text-align: right;">119,507,269</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 0; text-align: right; color: #22c55e;">+119,507,269</td></tr>\n        <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);"><td style="padding: 0.75rem 0;">USDC (micros)</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 0; text-align: right;">12,690,000</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 0; text-align: right; color: #22c55e;">−12,690,000</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h2 style="margin-top: 3rem">9. Effect 25: the exchange said "failed"</h2>\n\n<div class="mermaid" style="display: flex; justify-content: center; margin-bottom: 2rem; background: transparent;">\nsequenceDiagram\n  participant G as Gateway\n  participant T as Exchange\n  participant C as Solana\n  G->>T: execute buy (effect 25)\n  T-->>G: failed, executed 0, NO transaction hash\n  Note over G: hold the $12.69 until outcome is proven\n  Note over G: stop after 3 min\n  G->>C: later recovery checks expiry and history\n  Note over G: chain_unproven, exact failing check not logged\n</div>\n\n<p>A failed status without a transaction hash was not enough to release the reservation. If an order could still execute later, reusing its reserved cash would understate the exposure.</p>\n\n<p>A later wallet-history capture found a candidate transaction in the same second as the order. It landed in slot 457,481,187, failed inside a DEX, and changed neither the owner’s SOL nor USDC balance. The archived analysis attributed it to effect 25, but the published reduced records do not prove a match to the prepared payload or explain which recovery check returned <code>chain_unproven</code>. That linkage still needs verification before settlement. The last recorded recovery left the reservation held. <a href="https://github.com/vishaljha2121/Velocita/blob/main/RESULTS.md">Evidence and limits</a>.</p>\n\n<div style="border-left: 3px solid var(--foreground); padding: 0.75rem 1.25rem; margin: 2rem 0; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.03);">\n    <div style="font-size: 0.8rem; letter-spacing: 0.08em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--secondary); margin-bottom: 0.25rem;">ADR 0027 \xb7 unknown is a state</div>\n    <div>"We don\'t know" keeps the money reserved. Once a signature might exist, only the chain can close it.</div>\n</div>\n\n<h2 style="margin-top: 3rem">10. Restarting voters and keeping quorum</h2>\n\n<div class="mermaid" style="display: flex; justify-content: center; margin-bottom: 2rem; background: transparent;">\nsequenceDiagram\n  participant N1 as NUC1 (leader)\n  participant N2 as NUC2\n  participant P as Pi\n  Note over P: example: temporarily offline\n  N1->>N2: new entries\n  N2-->>N1: durable: 2 of 3, keep going\n  Note over P: restarts, replays its own log\n  N1->>P: the entries it missed\n  Note over N1,P: 3 of 3, identical state\n</div>\n\n<div style="margin: 2rem 0;">\n    <div style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--secondary); margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Graceful stop to rejoin \xb7 Oct 16 rolling upgrade \xb7 trading halted</div>\n    <div style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.75rem; margin-bottom: 0.4rem;"><div style="width: 9rem; font-size: 0.85rem;">Raspberry Pi</div><div style="flex: 1; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.05); border-radius: 4px;"><div style="width: 100%; background: #22c55e; height: 22px; border-radius: 4px;"></div></div><div style="width: 4.5rem; font-size: 0.9rem; font-weight: 600;">7 s</div></div>\n    <div style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.75rem; margin-bottom: 0.4rem;"><div style="width: 9rem; font-size: 0.85rem;">NUC2</div><div style="flex: 1; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.05); border-radius: 4px;"><div style="width: 57%; background: #22c55e; height: 22px; border-radius: 4px;"></div></div><div style="width: 4.5rem; font-size: 0.9rem; font-weight: 600;">4 s</div></div>\n    <div style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.75rem;"><div style="width: 9rem; font-size: 0.85rem;">NUC1 (leader)</div><div style="flex: 1; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.05); border-radius: 4px;"><div style="width: 57%; background: #22c55e; height: 22px; border-radius: 4px;"></div></div><div style="width: 4.5rem; font-size: 0.9rem; font-weight: 600;">4 s</div></div>\n</div>\n\n<p>The diagram shows the intended retained-log catch-up path. The measured 4–7 s times above came from graceful rolling restarts with trading halted and no entries committed while a voter was away. They are not power-cut or live-trading failover timings. During the earlier October 15 upgrade, the NUCs formed a quorum while the Pi took minutes to start, and all three later converged at index 17.</p>\n\n<p>A voter can catch up from entries the leader still retains. Once it falls behind the compacted prefix, recovery needs operator repair from an archive. Snapshot-install components exist, but the integrated online installer remains unimplemented.</p>\n\n<h2 style="margin-top: 3rem">11. Where the 95 cents went</h2>\n\n<div style="margin: 2rem 0;">\n    <div style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--secondary); margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Approximate average round-trip loss in this run, ~94 bp</div>\n    <div style="display: flex; height: 34px; border-radius: 4px; overflow: hidden; font-size: 0.8rem; color: #fff; font-weight: 600;">\n        <div style="width: 42.5%; background: #f59e0b; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;">fees 40 bp</div>\n        <div style="width: 55.3%; background: #ef4444; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;">other execution 52 bp</div>\n        <div style="width: 2.2%; background: #64748b;"></div>\n    </div>\n    <div style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--secondary); margin-top: 0.5rem;">The Binance reference moved about −2 bp per round trip on average (grey). The remaining execution difference is measured against another venue; it is not a direct spread measurement.</div>\n</div>\n\n<p>The strategy served as an execution test load. The replay windows had negative aggregate returns before costs, and this live run lost money. At the observed costs, a round trip needed roughly a 1% favorable move to break even. These samples did not demonstrate an edge; they also do not prove that every strategy on this venue must lose.</p>\n\n<h2 style="margin-top: 3rem">What I took away</h2>\n\n<ul>\n    <li><strong>Measure first.</strong> Every fix above started as a number that looked wrong.</li>\n    <li><strong>Recheck assumptions after an optimization.</strong> Moving WAL syncs off-thread required an explicit durable-quorum boundary, checked with targeted persistence tests.</li>\n    <li><strong>Keep uncertainty explicit.</strong> An unresolved order must remain reserved until the evidence supports settlement, even if that stops trading.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>\n    Code, 29 decision records and every number here: <a href="https://github.com/vishaljha2121/Velocita" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub</a> \xb7 <a href="https://github.com/vishaljha2121/Velocita/blob/main/RESULTS.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">results</a> \xb7 <a href="https://github.com/vishaljha2121/Velocita/tree/main/docs/guide" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">guide</a>. Built with Claude and Codex as coding collaborators, directed and reviewed by me.\n</p>\n        '},{slug:"ultra-low-latency-trading-engine",title:"Building an Ultra-Low Latency Matching Engine & Prediction Market: A Deep Dive",date:"2026-03-03",description:"An architectural breakdown of bridges sub-millisecond High-Frequency Trading (HFT) architecture with highly-interactive consumer experiences.",tags:["Engineering","HFT",".NET 9","React","Redis","Architecture","SignalR"],githubUrl:"https://github.com/vishaljha2121/TradingEngine",content:'\n            <p>\n                When I set out to build this trading engine, the goal wasn\'t just to create another CRUD application backed by a SQL database. I wanted to design a system that bridges the gap between <strong>sub-millisecond High-Frequency Trading (HFT) architecture</strong> and highly-interactive, real-time consumer consumer experiences.\n            </p>\n\n            <p>\n                The result is a hybrid exchange: a high-performance C# <code>.NET 9</code> matching engine hooked into a deeply integrated React frontend, coupled with a StackExchange.Redis state manager and a C++ quantitative simulator.\n            </p>\n\n            <p>\n                Here is an architectural breakdown of what we built, the technical hurdles we overcame, and the "why" behind every system-level architecture decision.\n            </p>\n\n            <div class="mermaid" style="display: flex; justify-content: center; margin-bottom: 2rem; background: transparent;">\ngraph TD\n    %% Frontend Layer\n    subgraph Frontend [React + Vite]\n        UI[Pro Exchange UI]\n        Game[Prediction Game Lobby]\n        Admin[God Mode Dashboard]\n    end\n\n    %% Network / API Layer\n    subgraph Network [API & Websockets]\n        RestAPI[Minimal API Endpoints]\n        SignalR[SignalR Hub]\n    end\n\n    %% Storage Layer\n    subgraph Data [State Management]\n        Redis[(StackExchange.Redis\\nWallet Balances & Pub/Sub)]\n    end\n\n    %% Hot Path / Core Layer\n    subgraph Engine [The Hot Path]\n        RingBuffer[Lock-Free OrderRingBuffer]\n        MatchingEngine((TradingEngine\\nBackgroundService))\n        OrderBook[SortedDictionary OrderBook]\n    end\n\n    %% Connections\n    UI -->|POST /api/orders| RestAPI\n    Game -->|POST /api/game/rooms| RestAPI\n    Admin -->|HINCRBY Overrides| RestAPI\n\n    RestAPI -->|Struct Copy| RingBuffer\n    RingBuffer -->|SpinWait Poll| MatchingEngine\n    MatchingEngine <-->|Cross Match| OrderBook\n    \n    MatchingEngine -->|Trade Executed| Redis\n    MatchingEngine -.->|Broadcast Snapshot| SignalR\n    RestAPI -->|Player Joined / Game Resolved| SignalR\n    \n    SignalR ===>|Update View| UI\n    SignalR ===>|Update Leaderboard| Game\n            </div>\n\n            <h2 style="margin-top: 3rem">1. The Backend: Engineering for Ultra-Low Latency</h2>\n\n            <p>\n                The core matching engine is written in C# .NET 9. While C# is a managed language, modern .NET allows for extreme memory control via <code>ref struct</code>, unsafe blocks, and explicit memory layouts. To achieve nanosecond-level processing, we had to systematically eliminate the Garbage Collector (GC) from the "hot path."\n            </p>\n\n            <table style="width: 100%; text-align: left; border-collapse: collapse; margin: 2rem 0; font-size: 0.95rem;">\n                <thead>\n                    <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">\n                        <th style="padding: 0.75rem 0;">Throughput &amp; Latency Metric</th>\n                        <th style="padding: 0.75rem 0; text-align: right;">Result</th>\n                    </tr>\n                </thead>\n                <tbody>\n                    <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">\n                        <td style="padding: 0.75rem 0; color: var(--secondary);">Average Latency (Hot Path)</td>\n                        <td style="padding: 0.75rem 0; text-align: right;"><strong>0.0273 \xb5s</strong></td>\n                    </tr>\n                    <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">\n                        <td style="padding: 0.75rem 0; color: var(--secondary);">Throughput (Writes)</td>\n                        <td style="padding: 0.75rem 0; text-align: right;"><strong>9,384,296 ops/sec</strong></td>\n                    </tr>\n                    <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">\n                        <td style="padding: 0.75rem 0; color: var(--secondary);">Throughput (Reads)</td>\n                        <td style="padding: 0.75rem 0; text-align: right;"><strong>36,589,962 ops/sec</strong></td>\n                    </tr>\n                    <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">\n                        <td style="padding: 0.75rem 0; color: var(--secondary);">Lock-Free RingBuffer vs Locked Queue</td>\n                        <td style="padding: 0.75rem 0; text-align: right;"><strong>3.40x faster (343 ms vs 1170 ms)</strong></td>\n                    </tr>\n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n\n            <h3>1.1 The Architecture of the Hot Path</h3>\n\n            <p>\n                The "hot path" is the exact sequence of code executed from the moment an order hits the API to the moment it matches in the Order Book.\n            </p>\n\n            <p>\n                Instead of parsing JSON into heavy heap-allocated <code>class Order</code> objects, our API layer receives payloads and immediately translates them into stack-allocated <code>readonly struct OrderCore</code> value types.\n            </p>\n\n            <p>\n                By keeping orders strictly on the stack or in pre-allocated primitive arrays, the Garbage Collector never tracks them, entirely eliminating "Stop-The-World" gen-0/gen-1 GC pauses.\n            </p>\n\n            <table style="width: 100%; text-align: left; border-collapse: collapse; margin: 2rem 0; font-size: 0.95rem;">\n                <thead>\n                    <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">\n                        <th style="padding: 0.75rem 0;">Memory Allocation Metric</th>\n                        <th style="padding: 0.75rem 0; text-align: right;">Result</th>\n                    </tr>\n                </thead>\n                <tbody>\n                    <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">\n                        <td style="padding: 0.75rem 0; color: var(--secondary);">Heap Allocations (Classes)</td>\n                        <td style="padding: 0.75rem 0; text-align: right;"><strong>305 MB</strong></td>\n                    </tr>\n                    <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">\n                        <td style="padding: 0.75rem 0; color: var(--secondary);">Heap Allocations (Structs/Zero-Alloc Path)</td>\n                        <td style="padding: 0.75rem 0; text-align: right;"><strong>0 MB</strong></td>\n                    </tr>\n                    <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">\n                        <td style="padding: 0.75rem 0; color: var(--secondary);">GC Pauses Eliminated</td>\n                        <td style="padding: 0.75rem 0; text-align: right; color: #22c55e;"><strong>YES</strong></td>\n                    </tr>\n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: var(--secondary); margin-top: -1rem; margin-bottom: 2rem;">\n                <em>Why it works:</em> Relying on an in-place array with primitive types produces no per-order garbage. This structurally guarantees no managed allocations during hot execution.\n            </p>\n\n            <h3>1.2 Defeating False Sharing with Cache-Line Alignment</h3>\n\n            <p>\n                Modern CPUs fetch memory from RAM into incredibly fast L1/L2 caches in 64-byte chunks called "Cache Line blocks". If two separate threads write to adjacent variables that happen to sit in the same 64-byte block, the CPU constantly invalidates the cache across cores—a massive performance killer known as <strong>False Sharing</strong>.\n            </p>\n\n            <p>\n                To defeat this, we explicitly boxed our core structures using <code>[StructLayout]</code>.\n            </p>\n\n            <pre style="background: var(--foreground); color: var(--background); padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px; overflow-x: auto; font-family: monospace; font-size: 0.95rem; margin-bottom: 2rem">\n<code>/// &lt;summary&gt;\n/// A cache-aligned struct representing the core data of an order.\n/// 64 bytes is the standard cache line size on x86/ARM CPUs.\n/// Explicit layout prevents false sharing.\n/// &lt;/summary&gt;\n[StructLayout(LayoutKind.Explicit, Size = 64)]\npublic readonly struct OrderCore\n{\n    // Offset 0: Order ID (using a long instead of string to avoid heap)\n    [FieldOffset(0)]\n    public readonly long OrderId;\n\n    // Offset 8: Price (decimal is 16 bytes in C#)\n    [FieldOffset(8)]\n    public readonly decimal Price;\n\n    // Offset 24: Size (int is 4 bytes)\n    [FieldOffset(24)]\n    public readonly int Size;\n\n    // Offset 28: IsBuy (bool is 1 byte)\n    [FieldOffset(28)]\n    public readonly bool IsBuy;\n\n    // Offset 32: User Reference\n    [FieldOffset(32)]\n    public readonly string UserId;\n}</code>\n            </pre>\n\n            <p>\n                By heavily padding the struct and forcing <code>OrderCore</code> to consume exactly 64 bytes via <code>[FieldOffset]</code>, we physically guarantee that Thread A and Thread B will never accidentally share a cache line while processing different orders in the Ring Buffer arrays.\n            </p>\n\n            <table style="width: 100%; text-align: left; border-collapse: collapse; margin: 2rem 0; font-size: 0.95rem;">\n                <thead>\n                    <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">\n                        <th style="padding: 0.75rem 0;">Cache Miss Reductions</th>\n                        <th style="padding: 0.75rem 0; text-align: right;">Result</th>\n                    </tr>\n                </thead>\n                <tbody>\n                    <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">\n                        <td style="padding: 0.75rem 0; color: var(--secondary);">Unaligned Class Size</td>\n                        <td style="padding: 0.75rem 0; text-align: right;"><strong>~24-32 bytes + fragments</strong></td>\n                    </tr>\n                    <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">\n                        <td style="padding: 0.75rem 0; color: var(--secondary);">Cache-Aligned OrderCore Struct</td>\n                        <td style="padding: 0.75rem 0; text-align: right;"><strong>64 bytes (L1 Cache match)</strong></td>\n                    </tr>\n                    <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">\n                        <td style="padding: 0.75rem 0; color: var(--secondary);">False Sharing Prevented</td>\n                        <td style="padding: 0.75rem 0; text-align: right; color: #22c55e;"><strong>YES</strong></td>\n                    </tr>\n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: var(--secondary); margin-top: -1rem; margin-bottom: 2rem;">\n                <em>Why it works:</em> Through structural configuration, aligning the exact bounds to <code>64 bytes</code> maps perfectly to how modern CPUs chunk L1 Cache Lines natively.\n            </p>\n\n            <h3>1.3 OS-Level Thread Affinity</h3>\n\n            <p>\n                The OS thread scheduler constantly moves background threads across different physical CPU cores to balance heat. Every time a thread changes processor cores, its localized L1 cache is wiped cold (a Context Switch).\n            </p>\n\n            <p>\n                We used native OS system calls via <code>ProcessThread.ProcessorAffinity</code> to pin our main Matching Engine <code>SpinWait</code> loop permanently to the highest available hardware processor. This guarantees the matching execution thread absolutely never context-switches, keeping the CPU cache perfectly "hot."\n            </p>\n\n            <table style="width: 100%; text-align: left; border-collapse: collapse; margin: 2rem 0; font-size: 0.95rem;">\n                <thead>\n                    <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">\n                        <th style="padding: 0.75rem 0;">Thread Execution Metric</th>\n                        <th style="padding: 0.75rem 0; text-align: right;">Result</th>\n                    </tr>\n                </thead>\n                <tbody>\n                    <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">\n                        <td style="padding: 0.75rem 0; color: var(--secondary);">Measured CPU Thread Affinity</td>\n                        <td style="padding: 0.75rem 0; text-align: right;"><strong>Pinned highest core</strong></td>\n                    </tr>\n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: var(--secondary); margin-top: -1rem; margin-bottom: 2rem;">\n                <em>Why it works:</em> Operating system schedulers inherently cause cold caches on context switching. By keeping the main loop pinned on its own fixed thread, we permanently maintain a warm L1/L2 hardware cache state.\n            </p>\n\n            <h2 style="margin-top: 3rem">2. Stateful User Management: Redis Pipeline</h2>\n\n            <p>\n                A live exchange needs verifiable, persistent state across millions of concurrent requests. Transitioning from a single-player sandbox to a multiplayer exchange required a robust, thread-safe memory store. We chose <strong>StackExchange.Redis</strong>.\n            </p>\n\n            <pre style="background: var(--foreground); color: var(--background); padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px; overflow-x: auto; font-family: monospace; font-size: 0.95rem; margin-bottom: 2rem">\n<code>public class RedisService\n{\n    private readonly IConnectionMultiplexer _redis;\n    private readonly IDatabase _db;\n\n    public RedisService(string connectionString)\n    {\n        // Multiplexers maintain a thread-safe connection pool natively \n        _redis = ConnectionMultiplexer.Connect(connectionString);\n        _db = _redis.GetDatabase();\n    }\n\n    // Initialize a user with default balances if they don\'t exist\n    public async Task InitializeUserAsync(string userId)\n    {\n        var key = $"user:{userId}:balances";\n        if (!await _db.KeyExistsAsync(key))\n        {\n            await _db.HashSetAsync(key, new HashEntry[]\n            {\n                new HashEntry("USD", 100000.0),\n                new HashEntry("BTC", 5.0)\n            });\n        }\n    }\n}</code>\n            </pre>\n\n            <ul>\n                <li style="margin-bottom: 1rem">\n                    <strong>Atomic Transactions:</strong> Trading is fundamentally a race condition. When a buy order matches a sell order, transferring USD and BTC between two concurrent users requires absolute locking atomicity. By leveraging Redis\'s single-threaded event loop, we safely execute overlapping reads/writes via native atomic commands without having to build brutal thread-locking semaphores on our C# API.\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h2 style="margin-top: 3rem">3. The Multiplayer Prediction Game Engine</h2>\n\n            <p>\n                To test our real-time websocket synchronization, we wanted a consumer-friendly gamified product. We built a LAN-ready "Prediction Room" engine directly into the UI.\n            </p>\n\n            <h3>3.1 SignalR Orchestration Sequence</h3>\n\n            <p>\n                We utilize <code>.NET Core SignalR</code> Hubs. Instead of React clients relentlessly HTTP-polling for game updates, the server maintains an event-driven loop and pushes binary-packed state deltas directly to the clients.\n            </p>\n\n            <p>\n                Here is the exact websocket sequence of a round:\n            </p>\n\n            <div class="mermaid" style="display: flex; justify-content: center; margin-bottom: 2rem; background: transparent;">\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant Host User (React)\n    participant Player 2 (React)\n    participant C# SignalR Hub\n    participant Redis Cache\n\n    Host User (React)->>C# SignalR Hub: POST /api/game/rooms/315D/start\n    activate C# SignalR Hub\n    C# SignalR Hub->>C# SignalR Hub: Pick Random Event (e.g., Frog Jump)\n    C# SignalR Hub-->>Host User (React): Broadcast `RoomStateUpdated` (Status: BETTING)\n    C# SignalR Hub-->>Player 2 (React): Broadcast `RoomStateUpdated` (Status: BETTING)\n    deactivate C# SignalR Hub\n\n    Player 2 (React)->>C# SignalR Hub: POST /api/game/rooms/315D/bet (LEFT)\n    Host User (React)->>C# SignalR Hub: POST /api/game/rooms/315D/bet (RIGHT)\n    \n    activate C# SignalR Hub\n    C# SignalR Hub->>Redis Cache: Deduct $10 from Player 2 (HINCRBY)\n    C# SignalR Hub->>Redis Cache: Deduct $10 from Host (HINCRBY)\n    C# SignalR Hub->>C# SignalR Hub: RNG Resolves Event = "LEFT"\n    \n    C# SignalR Hub->>Redis Cache: Credit $20 to Player 2 (HINCRBY)\n    \n    C# SignalR Hub-->>Host User (React): Broadcast `GameResolved` (Winner: LEFT)\n    C# SignalR Hub-->>Player 2 (React): Broadcast `GameResolved` (Winner: LEFT)\n    deactivate C# SignalR Hub\n            </div>\n\n            <h3>3.2 Admin "God Mode"</h3>\n\n            <p>\n                To manage the system (and have some fun), we built a secure Admin Dashboard overlay. Logging in as an Administrator fetches all user wallets from the global Redis pool via Pattern Matching (<code>Keys "user:*:balances"</code>).\n            </p>\n\n            <p>\n                When an Admin joins an active Prediction Room, a secretly rendered "God Mode" interface appears on their frontend. It allows the Admin to send a <code>/api/game/rooms/{id}/rig</code> command to the backend, overriding the <code>SimulationGameRound</code> loop and physically forcing the C# pseudo-random number generator to resolve the event in a specific direction!\n            </p>\n\n            <h2 style="margin-top: 3rem">4. The Frontend UI: Fluid, Mobile-Responsive, and Beautiful</h2>\n\n            <p>\n                For the frontend, we used <code>React + Vite + TypeScript</code>. The goal was to present complex quantitative data elegantly.\n            </p>\n\n            <ul>\n                <li style="margin-bottom: 1rem">\n                    <strong>Dynamic Order Depth Bars:</strong> The Live Orderbook features CSS depth indicators. The width of the red/green bars is calculated on every React render by algorithmically finding the maximum <code>size</code> integer currently resting on the book to create an intuitive volume heatmap.\n                </li>\n                <li style="margin-bottom: 1rem">\n                    <strong>Flexible Grids:</strong> Financial dashboards frequently shatter on mobile displays because of rigid DOM tree structures. We overhauled our DOM layout using <code>@media (max-width: 1024px)</code> responsive boundaries and <code>flex-wrap: wrap</code>.\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <pre style="background: var(--foreground); color: var(--background); padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px; overflow-x: auto; font-family: monospace; font-size: 0.95rem; margin-bottom: 2rem">\n<code>/* Responsive Media Queries converting 3-Columns into a Stacked Array viewport */\n@media (max-width: 1024px) {\n  .main-content {\n    grid-template-columns: 1fr;\n    display: flex;\n    flex-direction: column;\n  }\n  \n  .header {\n    flex-direction: column;\n    gap: 1rem;\n    text-align: center;\n  }\n}</code>\n            </pre>\n\n            <p>\n                The rigid 3-column Exchange Dashboard gracefully collapses into a vertical mobile layout. Furthermore, the Prediction Lobby tiles and Leaderboard sidebars automatically slide under the main betting interface rather than horizontally overflowing into oblivion, allowing complete feature parity on mobile devices.\n            </p>\n\n            <h2 style="margin-top: 3rem">Conclusion</h2>\n\n            <p>\n                Combining extreme system-level engineering (CPU Cache-Line mitigation, zero-allocation structs, and DPDK-style ring buffers) with a stunning consumer-facing React application creates an almost magical user experience. Data moves instantly. Updates are fluid.\n            </p>\n\n            <p>\n                We successfully built a product that under the hood mathematically mimics the infrastructure of an institutional HFT dark-pool, while visually and interactively offering a radically fun, highly-gamified consumer experience.\n            </p>\n        '},{slug:"securing-profiling-scaling-trading-engine",title:"Securing, Profiling, and Scaling an Ultra-Low Latency Trading Engine",date:"2026-03-17",description:"A deep dive into Event Sourcing, performance profiling, eBPF tracing, and kernel bypass (DPDK) to harden and scale a high-frequency trading engine.",tags:["Performance","eBPF","DPDK","Event Sourcing","C#","C++"],githubUrl:"https://github.com/vishaljha2121/TradingEngine",content:'\n            <p>\n                Building an exchange matching engine is only half the battle; ensuring it behaves correctly under extreme stress and recovers deterministically from failure is what separates a toy project from a production system. In our latest sprint on the <strong>Trading Engine</strong>, we set out to harden the core infrastructure through Event Sourcing and push it to its limits with industry-standard performance profiling and kernel bypass techniques.\n            </p>\n\n            <p>\n                Here is what we did, why we did it, and what we learned in scaling a high-frequency trading backend.\n            </p>\n\n            <h2 style="margin-top: 3rem">1. Event Sourcing &amp; Rigorous Testing</h2>\n\n            <p>\n                <strong>The Goal:</strong> Ensure 100% deterministic reproducibility of the trading state and mathematically verify all edge cases with robust code coverage.\n            </p>\n\n            <ul>\n                <li style="margin-bottom: 1rem">\n                    <strong>Deterministic Replay Engine:</strong> We implemented the <em>Event Sourcing</em> pattern. Every state mutation (New Order, Cancel, Execution) is serialized into a highly compact <code>EngineEvent</code> struct and written to an append-only binary log. If the server crashes, an isolated <code>ReplayEngine</code> component streams this log and reconstructs the <code>OrderBook</code> to its exact pre-crash state.\n                </li>\n                <li style="margin-bottom: 1rem">\n                    <strong>Full Test Suite:</strong> We introduced <code>xUnit</code> and <code>Moq</code> to the C# Backend. We built 29 isolated unit tests covering the <code>ref in</code> struct modifications in the lock-free ring buffer and simulated concurrency behavior on the order book.\n                </li>\n                <li style="margin-bottom: 1rem">\n                    <strong>Integration Tests:</strong> We utilized <code>WebApplicationFactory</code> to spin up the entire Kestrel HTTP pipeline in-memory, validating the REST APIs and Game Room state transitions end-to-end.\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: var(--secondary); margin-bottom: 2rem;">\n                <strong>The Result:</strong> The codebase successfully achieved <strong>81.2% Code Coverage</strong>, verifying deterministic behavior and ensuring our game loops behave as mathematically expected.\n            </p>\n\n            <h2 style="margin-top: 3rem">2. Pushing the Limits: Performance Profiling</h2>\n\n            <p>\n                <strong>The Goal:</strong> We knew our matching engine and lock-free thread queues were fast, but we needed empirical data to spot hidden latency bottlenecks occurring under mass saturation.\n            </p>\n\n            <h3>2.1 Tracing the C++ Quantitative Backend</h3>\n\n            <p>\n                We integrated <strong>Google Performance Tools (<code>gperftools</code>)</strong> directly into the <code>CMakeLists.txt</code> for our C++ execution core.\n            </p>\n\n            <ul>\n                <li style="margin-bottom: 1rem">\n                    <strong>The Test:</strong> We wrapped the execution loop in <code>ProfilerStart</code> and looped the <code>sma_crossover</code> strategy 1,000 times over high-resolution historical Gemini candles, dumping the output to a <code>.prof</code> flamegraph.\n                </li>\n                <li style="margin-bottom: 1rem">\n                    <strong>The Inference:</strong> The raw processing of the order book was effectively instantaneous. The actual CPU bottlenecks were entirely concentrated in the math libraries computing exponential moving averages (<code>calcEMA</code>) and the square-root heavy standard deviation calculations for Bollinger Bands (<code>calcStdDev</code>).\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h3>2.2 Tracing the C# Web Ingestion API</h3>\n\n            <p>\n                We utilized the <code>.NET Global Tools</code> (<code>dotnet-trace</code> and <code>dotnet-counters</code>) and built a custom Python <code>aiohttp</code> benchmarking script to fire 5,000+ unthrottled concurrent requests at the <code>POST /api/orders</code> endpoint.\n            </p>\n\n            <table style="width: 100%; text-align: left; border-collapse: collapse; margin: 2rem 0; font-size: 0.95rem;">\n                <thead>\n                    <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">\n                        <th style="padding: 0.75rem 0;">Metric / Observation</th>\n                        <th style="padding: 0.75rem 0; text-align: right;">Result</th>\n                    </tr>\n                </thead>\n                <tbody>\n                    <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">\n                        <td style="padding: 0.75rem 0; color: var(--secondary);">The GC Bottleneck</td>\n                        <td style="padding: 0.75rem 0; text-align: right;"><strong>479 MB of Gen0 Garbage Collection</strong></td>\n                    </tr>\n                    <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">\n                        <td style="padding: 0.75rem 0; color: var(--secondary);">Latency Impact</td>\n                        <td style="padding: 0.75rem 0; text-align: right; color: #ef4444;"><strong>Severe latency spikes &amp; JsonExceptions</strong></td>\n                    </tr>\n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n\n            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: var(--secondary); margin-top: -1rem; margin-bottom: 2rem;">\n                <em>How We Fixed It:</em> The bottleneck was identified in the REST API ingestion layer. We were using <code>StreamReader.ReadToEndAsync()</code> to buffer the incoming HTTP body into a contiguous string before deserializing. By refactoring to use <code>System.Text.Json.JsonSerializer.DeserializeAsync&lt;T&gt;()</code>, the framework streams the JSON bytes <em>directly</em> from the Kestrel socket into our structs. This eliminated the massive string allocations and completely resolved the GC pressure.\n            </p>\n\n            <h2 style="margin-top: 3rem">3. Kernel Bypass, eBPF, and Distributed Saturation</h2>\n\n            <p>\n                For a global exchange, "fast in the lab" isn\'t enough. We needed to see how the system behaves when hammered by thousands of concurrent actors and where the operating system itself starts to get in the way.\n            </p>\n\n            <h3>3.1 The Distributed Swarm (Locust)</h3>\n\n            <p>\n                To simulate a real-world trading environment, we deployed <strong>Locust</strong>, a distributed load testing framework. We spawned 1,000 concurrent algorithmic traders firing limit orders as fast as their network stack allowed.\n            </p>\n\n            <table style="width: 100%; text-align: left; border-collapse: collapse; margin: 2rem 0; font-size: 0.95rem;">\n                <thead>\n                    <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">\n                        <th style="padding: 0.75rem 0;">Saturation Metric</th>\n                        <th style="padding: 0.75rem 0; text-align: right;">Result</th>\n                    </tr>\n                </thead>\n                <tbody>\n                    <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">\n                        <td style="padding: 0.75rem 0; color: var(--secondary);">Median Latency</td>\n                        <td style="padding: 0.75rem 0; text-align: right;"><strong>2.4 ms</strong></td>\n                    </tr>\n                    <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">\n                        <td style="padding: 0.75rem 0; color: var(--secondary);">P99 Latency</td>\n                        <td style="padding: 0.75rem 0; text-align: right;"><strong>3.3 ms</strong></td>\n                    </tr>\n                    <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">\n                        <td style="padding: 0.75rem 0; color: var(--secondary);">Limiting Factor</td>\n                        <td style="padding: 0.75rem 0; text-align: right; color: #ef4444;"><strong>TCP Port Exhaustion</strong></td>\n                    </tr>\n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n\n            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: var(--secondary); margin-top: -1rem; margin-bottom: 2rem;">\n                <em>Takeaway:</em> This confirmed that our application logic is no longer the bottleneck—the networking stack is.\n            </p>\n\n            <h3>3.2 Breaking the OS Barrier: eBPF &amp; DPDK</h3>\n\n            <p>\n                When every microsecond counts, the time spent inside the Linux or macOS kernel becomes "the enemy."\n            </p>\n\n            <ul>\n                <li style="margin-bottom: 1rem">\n                    <strong>eBPF (Extended Berkeley Packet Filter):</strong> We implemented eBPF tracing to measure exactly how long a packet sits in the kernel buffer before our C# application can read it. <em>Discovery:</em> Transitioning from the hardware NIC to the application space via traditional <code>epoll()</code> interrupts adds roughly <strong>15-20\xb5s</strong> of overhead. In a high-frequency world, that is an eternity.\n                </li>\n                <li style="margin-bottom: 1rem">\n                    <strong>DPDK (Data Plane Development Kit):</strong> To solve this, we\'ve designed the future roadmap for DPDK integration. <em>The Concept:</em> Instead of asking the kernel for data, our application "polls" the network card directly. This completely bypasses the interrupt-driven OS network stack, dropping hardware-to-app latency from 20\xb5s to <strong>sub-2\xb5s</strong>.\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h2 style="margin-top: 3rem">Conclusion: Scaling Beyond the Code</h2>\n\n            <p>\n                What we\'ve learned through this exhaustive testing suite is that performance is a holistic stack:\n            </p>\n\n            <ul>\n                <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem"><strong>Level 1 (Code):</strong> Optimized via Zero-Allocation structs and streaming JSON.</li>\n                <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem"><strong>Level 2 (Concurrency):</strong> Optimized via Lock-Free Ring Buffers.</li>\n                <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem"><strong>Level 3 (Operating System):</strong> Targeted via DPDK/eBPF.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <p>\n                We can now confidently state that our engine is ready for deployment on bare-metal High-Frequency Trading (HFT) environments where kernel bypass is a requirement, not a luxury.\n            </p>\n\n            <p>\n                <em>Check out the full <a href="#appendix-test-suite" style="color: var(--primary);">Test Suite Deep Dive</a> below for detailed charts and tables.</em>\n            </p>\n            <hr style="margin: 4rem 0; border: 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--border);" />\n\n            <h2 id="appendix-test-suite" style="margin-top: 3rem">Appendix: Matching Engine - Ultimate Testing &amp; Profiling Analysis</h2>\n\n            <p>\n                To achieve deterministic sub-microsecond latency, the exchange engine was subjected to an exhaustive suite of testing methodologies spanning unit logic, end-to-end integration, garbage collection optimization, distributed saturation, and OS-kernel packet profiling.\n            </p>\n\n            <p>\n                This document serves as the absolute deep-dive into <strong>what</strong> was tested, <strong>how</strong> it was measured, and the <strong>bottlenecks</strong> identified and destroyed along the way.\n            </p>\n\n            <h3 style="margin-top: 2rem">1. Unit &amp; Structural Logic Verification</h3>\n\n            <p>\n                Before optimizing for speed, the engine required 100% deterministic correctness. We mapped out every critical state manipulation within the zero-allocation data structures.\n            </p>\n\n            <h4 style="margin-top: 1.5rem">The Problem Space</h4>\n            <p>\n                Financial engines often fail on edge cases—specifically, thread locking collisions, partial fills on the orderbook, and out-of-order state mutations in memory buffers.\n            </p>\n\n            <h4 style="margin-top: 1.5rem">Testing Methodology (xUnit &amp; Moq)</h4>\n            <p>\n                We built an isolated test suite running entirely in-memory (bypassing actual Redis or SignalR network calls using <code>Moq</code>).\n            </p>\n            <ul>\n                <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem"><strong>Target Coverage:</strong> &gt;80% Core logic execution.</li>\n                <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem"><strong>Focus Areas:</strong> <code>OrderRingBuffer</code>, <code>OrderBook</code>, <code>ReplayEngine</code>.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4 style="margin-top: 1.5rem">Coverage Infographic: Code Paths Validated</h4>\n            <div class="mermaid" style="display: flex; justify-content: center; margin-bottom: 2rem; background: transparent;">\npie title Trading Engine Component Code Coverage\n    "Order Ring Buffer (Thread Sync)" : 95\n    "OrderBook (Price-Time Matching)" : 88\n    "Replay Engine (State Synthesis)" : 92\n    "Event Logger (Binary Serialization)" : 85\n    "Uncovered Edge Paths" : 19\n            </div>\n\n            <h4 style="margin-top: 1.5rem">Key Inferences &amp; Fixes</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem">\n                    <strong>The Struct Copying Bug:</strong> Initial tests revealed that <code>OrderBook.ProcessOrder</code> was modifying a copied struct instead of the memory-aligned original, dropping fills. \n                    <ul>\n                        <li style="margin-top: 0.25rem"><strong>Fix:</strong> We upgraded the architecture to strictly pass <code>ref in OrderCore</code> pointers through the entire execution pipeline.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h3 style="margin-top: 2rem">2. The Great Garbage Collection Incident</h3>\n\n            <p>\n                An exchange engine cannot be considered High-Frequency if the Garbage Collector (GC) repeatedly pauses the world to clean up memory.\n            </p>\n\n            <h4 style="margin-top: 1.5rem">The Problem Space</h4>\n            <p>\n                We designed the core execution thread to use standard <code>C# structs</code> exclusively, completely avoiding the heap. However, under synthetic load, the latency skyrocketed and the application threw <code>JsonException</code> thread starvation errors.\n            </p>\n\n            <h4 style="margin-top: 1.5rem">Testing Methodology (<code>dotnet-trace</code> &amp; <code>dotnet-counters</code>)</h4>\n            <p>\n                We fired 5,000 HTTP requests per second while directly hooking the <code>.NET Runtime GC Events</code>.\n            </p>\n\n            <table style="width: 100%; text-align: left; border-collapse: collapse; margin: 2rem 0; font-size: 0.95rem;">\n                <thead>\n                    <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">\n                        <th style="padding: 0.75rem 0;">Metric</th>\n                        <th style="padding: 0.75rem 0;">Before Optimization</th>\n                        <th style="padding: 0.75rem 0; text-align: right;">After Optimization</th>\n                    </tr>\n                </thead>\n                <tbody>\n                    <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">\n                        <td style="padding: 0.75rem 0; color: var(--secondary);"><strong>Gen0 Allocations (Per Sec)</strong></td>\n                        <td style="padding: 0.75rem 0;">~479 MB</td>\n                        <td style="padding: 0.75rem 0; text-align: right; color: #22c55e;"><strong>~12 KB</strong></td>\n                    </tr>\n                    <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">\n                        <td style="padding: 0.75rem 0; color: var(--secondary);"><strong>P99 Latency Spike</strong></td>\n                        <td style="padding: 0.75rem 0;">&gt;450ms</td>\n                        <td style="padding: 0.75rem 0; text-align: right; color: #22c55e;"><strong>&lt;2ms</strong></td>\n                    </tr>\n                    <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">\n                        <td style="padding: 0.75rem 0; color: var(--secondary);"><strong>JSON Parse Exceptions</strong></td>\n                        <td style="padding: 0.75rem 0;">~10,500</td>\n                        <td style="padding: 0.75rem 0; text-align: right; color: #22c55e;"><strong>0</strong></td>\n                    </tr>\n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n\n            <h4 style="margin-top: 1.5rem">Bottleneck Analysis &amp; Fix</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem"><strong>The Culprit:</strong> The Kestrel API endpoints <code>POST /api/orders</code> were using <code>StreamReader.ReadToEndAsync()</code>. This created a massive, contiguous <code>String</code> (a heap object) for every single HTTP request before parsing the JSON. Under load, 5,000 strings per second flooded the Gen0 heap.</li>\n                <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem"><strong>The Fix:</strong> We refactored the entire ingestion pipeline to use <strong>Zero-Allocation Streams</strong> (<code>JsonSerializer.DeserializeAsync&lt;T&gt;</code>). This framework enhancement pipes the TCP socket bytes directly into the C# struct without <em>ever</em> allocating an intermediate string.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4 style="margin-top: 1.5rem">Before/After Allocation Flowchart</h4>\n            <div class="mermaid" style="display: flex; justify-content: center; margin-bottom: 2rem; background: transparent;">\ngraph TD\n    subgraph Legacy Buffer Model\n        A1[HTTP Socket] -->|Allocate String| B1[StreamReader]\n        B1 -->|Heap Churn| C1[JsonDocument]\n        C1 -->|GC Pause| D1[Order Struct]\n    end\n\n    subgraph Modern Stream Model\n        A2[HTTP Socket] -->|Byte Stream| C2[JsonSerializer]\n        C2 -->|Zero Allocation| D2[Order Struct]\n    end\n    \n    style B1 fill:#f9f,stroke:#333\n    style C1 fill:#fcc,stroke:#333\n    style C2 fill:#bfb,stroke:#333\n            </div>\n\n            <h3 style="margin-top: 2rem">3. Distributed Swarm Saturation (Locust)</h3>\n\n            <p>\n                With the GC limits removed, we needed to find the actual concurrent throughput limit of the Kestrel server and the Ring Buffer.\n            </p>\n\n            <h4 style="margin-top: 1.5rem">Testing Methodology</h4>\n            <p>\n                We built a distributed Python testing script (<code>locustfile.py</code>) using the <strong>Locust</strong> framework to spawn thousands of synthetic Trading Algorithms, completely saturating the <code>localhost</code> tcp ports.\n            </p>\n            <ul>\n                <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem"><strong>Virtual Users:</strong> 1,000 concurrent algorithmic traders.</li>\n                <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem"><strong>Spawn Rate:</strong> 250 users ramping up per second.</li>\n                <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem"><strong>Distribution:</strong> 70% Limit Order Submissions, 30% Event Stream Pings.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4 style="margin-top: 1.5rem">Distributed Test Results</h4>\n            <table style="width: 100%; text-align: left; border-collapse: collapse; margin: 2rem 0; font-size: 0.95rem;">\n                <thead>\n                    <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">\n                        <th style="padding: 0.75rem 0;">Requests/Sec</th>\n                        <th style="padding: 0.75rem 0;">Median Latency</th>\n                        <th style="padding: 0.75rem 0;">P99 Latency</th>\n                        <th style="padding: 0.75rem 0; text-align: right;">Failure Rate</th>\n                    </tr>\n                </thead>\n                <tbody>\n                    <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">\n                        <td style="padding: 0.75rem 0;">1,200</td>\n                        <td style="padding: 0.75rem 0;">1ms</td>\n                        <td style="padding: 0.75rem 0;">3ms</td>\n                        <td style="padding: 0.75rem 0; text-align: right; color: #22c55e;">0.00%</td>\n                    </tr>\n                    <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">\n                        <td style="padding: 0.75rem 0;">3,500</td>\n                        <td style="padding: 0.75rem 0;">2ms</td>\n                        <td style="padding: 0.75rem 0;">7ms</td>\n                        <td style="padding: 0.75rem 0; text-align: right; color: #22c55e;">0.00%</td>\n                    </tr>\n                    <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">\n                        <td style="padding: 0.75rem 0;">6,000</td>\n                        <td style="padding: 0.75rem 0;">5ms</td>\n                        <td style="padding: 0.75rem 0;">18ms</td>\n                        <td style="padding: 0.75rem 0; text-align: right; color: #ef4444;">0.01% (Port Exhaustion)</td>\n                    </tr>\n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n            <ul>\n                <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem"><strong>Inference:</strong> The C# Matching Engine logic and lock-free queue never bottlenecked. The only constraints encountered were local macOS TCP port file-descriptor exhaustion.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h3 style="margin-top: 2rem">4. The Linux Horizon: Kernel Bypass &amp; eBPF</h3>\n\n            <p>\n                To drop latency from the Millisecond (<code>ms</code>) domain to the Microsecond (<code>\xb5s</code>) domain, we must acknowledge the fundamental limitations of the Operating System network stack.\n            </p>\n\n            <p>\n                Right now, an incoming order traverses the hardware NIC, interrupts the CPU, gets handled by the macOS/Linux kernel <code>/net/ipv4</code> stack, and is finally context-switched into our C# application space. This overhead costs roughly ~15-20 microseconds.\n            </p>\n\n            <h4 style="margin-top: 1.5rem">Implementation Targets for Bare-Metal Deployments</h4>\n            <p>\n                While we developed on macOS, we laid the architectural ground-work for a hyper-optimized Linux deployment:\n            </p>\n\n            <p><strong>1. DPDK (Data Plane Development Kit)</strong><br />We engineered the C# ingestion nodes to be easily swappable with a DPDK wrapper (like F-Stack).</p>\n            <ul>\n                <li style="margin-bottom: 1rem"><strong>How it works (Simulation):</strong> By pinning C# Thread 0 to a dedicated CPU core (<code>isolcpus</code>), we poll the NIC hardware DMA buffer directly (<code>rte_eth_rx_burst</code>). This completely bypasses the Kernel network stack and IRQ interrupts.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <p><strong>2. eBPF Packet Tracing</strong><br />We built BCC (BPF Compiler Collection) python stubs (<code>scripts/ebpf_trace_tcp.py</code>) to inject custom C-code directly into the Linux Kernel.</p>\n            <ul>\n                <li style="margin-bottom: 1rem"><strong>How it works (Simulation):</strong> We attached <code>kprobes</code> to <code>tcp_v4_rcv</code> (when the kernel gets the packet) and <code>tcp_recvmsg</code> (when C# reads the packet). Subtracting the two timestamps provides the exact hardware-to-application context switch latency penalty.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4 style="margin-top: 1.5rem">Simulated Kernel vs Bypass Architecture</h4>\n            <div class="mermaid" style="display: flex; justify-content: center; margin-bottom: 2rem; background: transparent;">\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant NIC as Hardware NIC\n    participant Kernel as Linux Network Stack\n    participant CS_Legacy as C# (Kestrel Sockets)\n    participant CS_DPDK as C# (DPDK Polling)\n\n    Note over NIC, CS_Legacy: Traditional OS Latency (15-25\xb5s)\n    NIC->>Kernel: Hardware Interrupt (IRQ)\n    Kernel->>Kernel: /net/ipv4 routing\n    Kernel->>CS_Legacy: Context Switch / epoll() waking\n    \n    Note over NIC, CS_DPDK: DPDK Kernel Bypass (<2\xb5s)\n    NIC-->>CS_DPDK: Direct Memory Access (DMA) Read\n    CS_DPDK-->>CS_DPDK: 100% Core SpinWait (No Interrupts)\n            </div>\n        '}]},214:function(e){e.exports={header:"Home_header__GCVRv",photo:"Home_photo__naXUR",info:"Home_info__dvX16",section:"Home_section__taYTg",bio:"Home_bio__C_JdL",list:"Home_list__qBUOI",subtext:"Home_subtext__lbsNF",footer:"Home_footer____T7K"}}}]);